WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:16.379
Tim Ferriss: David, so lovely to see you.

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:18.480
David Maisel: It's good to see you, Tim.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:25.710
Tim Ferriss: I had, thanks to your inspiration,
a fine matcha before sitting down and have

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:26.710
this conversation.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:27.710
Thank you for that.

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:31.110
David Maisel: I'm honored to inspire you on
something like that.

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:32.800
You inspire so many.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.720
Tim Ferriss: I'm putting together the coffee,
matcha alternating schedule, which seems to

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:39.890
be working very well for me so far.

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:40.930
Thank you for that.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:46.890
I'm excited to have you on this show for many
reasons.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:52.930
One of which is this is in a way a debut,
an audio long form debut.

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:59.559
I'm very thrilled to be able to explore a
lot of these things that will be scratching

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:04.250
my own itch in terms of asking questions that
follow my curiosity.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:10.800
Let's begin, not exactly at the beginning,
not at conception, but a beginning of sorts,

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.970
and that is with Michael Ovitz.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:17.090
How did you first connect with Michael Ovitz?

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:18.090
Who is Michael?

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:26.390
David Maisel: I mean, in the mid-1990s, Michael
Ovitz was the most powerful person in Hollywood/entertainment

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:28.569
by far.

00:01:28.569 --> 00:01:35.220
He had created Creative Artists Agency, which
was already, at that time, the biggest talent

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.409
agency in Hollywood.

00:01:37.409 --> 00:01:43.030
He had created this leverage by controlling
so many stars and by being this very mysterious

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:50.470
figure that he just became almost like The
Wizard of Oz, some mythical creature.

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:53.610
I remember I was debating a career switch.

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:58.780
I was working in consulting for entertainment
companies with Boston Consulting Group at

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:06.119
the time, and decided that my love of Hollywood
and movies, I had to move out to Hollywood

00:02:06.119 --> 00:02:09.080
and really try it and not have regrets.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:12.070
He was on the cover of Businessweek and I
just cold called him.

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:19.640
I sent him a letter, old-fashioned paper resume,
went to Kinko's to print it out, and I was

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:25.130
granted a five-minute interview and flown
out to L.A. That was the first meeting.

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:31.190
I remember he put me up at the Beverly Hills
Hilton and he made me stay two days waiting

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:32.190
for the meeting.

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:34.410
It just kept getting delayed.

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:41.900
Tim Ferriss: To dig into a few aspects of
that, we're talking about The Wizard of Oz

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:42.900
at the time.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:43.900
David Maisel: Right.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:51.610
Tim Ferriss: Wizard of Oz in Entertainment,
a power broker of high order.

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:56.310
He must have been having everyone and their
grandma and cousin trying to get a hold of

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:57.310
him.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:01.890
What did you say in your cover letter, or
what was it in your resume?

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:06.590
What combination of factors do you think led
to you being granted an audience with The

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:07.590
Wizard of Oz?

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:11.470
David Maisel: It's a good question because
I think I pieced it together after a while.

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:18.400
I was fortunate to have a stellar resume,
but so do a lot of people that I'm sure were

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:21.310
approaching Michael at the time.

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:31.370
It was in retrospect, I think I can say this
now, Michael had been hired by Matsushita,

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:37.780
the Japanese company, to secretly analyze
and potentially sell their holding of Universal

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:41.030
Studios, which they had bought years earlier.

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:44.590
I was in the right place at the right time.

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:50.760
As a great agent would do, he sold that assignment,
but he didn't necessarily have people that

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:52.860
could do the work for him.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:58.450
All of a sudden, he got this Harvard MBA resume
of some guy saying he'd be willing to work

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:00.980
and take whatever.

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:05.110
In many ways, I think it was just a really
fortunate timing that it came across his desk

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:06.110
then.

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:12.799
Tim Ferriss: You had the relevant experience
in mergers and acquisitions or that type of

00:04:12.799 --> 00:04:14.579
deal analysis or no?

00:04:14.579 --> 00:04:15.579
David Maisel: No.

00:04:15.579 --> 00:04:23.320
I sort of joke, I mean, I was very into creative
things and I turned that part of my brain

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:29.139
off after graduating from Duke because I needed
to make some money and I figured my rational

00:04:29.139 --> 00:04:31.210
brain was more predictable.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:37.190
I went to HBS and I had the Harvard Business
School moniker.

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:39.620
I had a couple years at Boston Consulting
Group.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:44.759
I think once I learned Michael, and once I
started working with him, that was the first

00:04:44.759 --> 00:04:50.419
job, which was to secretly help him on that
assignment, which was amazing as your first

00:04:50.419 --> 00:04:56.340
job in Hollywood to have this huge secret
that you couldn't share with anybody in town.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:00.100
I had this office way in the back so none
of the other agents would know what I was

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:01.100
doing.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:05.889
I was getting these secret emails between
Universal and the Japanese at the time that

00:05:05.889 --> 00:05:11.229
Lew Wasserman, another mythical figure, was
not even privy to.

00:05:11.229 --> 00:05:12.229
It was a blast.

00:05:12.229 --> 00:05:19.670
I think he enjoyed having his token Harvard
MBA by his side that he could offer up to

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:21.410
his clients.

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:22.410
He did.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:30.479
I was there for whatever that moniker might
deem to be useful for, whether it's Tom Cruise

00:05:30.479 --> 00:05:33.890
or Warren Beatty or a company like Matsushita.

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:36.020
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:45.730
Now, if we take a step back, and since you
came to know Michael very well, you look at

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:53.560
what made Michael effective as a power broker,
what are some of the ingredients that made

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:55.650
him so effective?

00:05:55.650 --> 00:06:01.350
Your description, as we led into this, made
me think of perhaps other comparables.

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:06.070
I'm sure very different people, very different
businesses would say like Larry Gagosian in

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:12.350
the art world or others who in these fairly
opaque, somewhat opaque industries just develop

00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:18.700
relationships that give them an incredible
degree of leverage.

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:23.699
What are some of the sort of requisite components,
ingredients that made him so good at what

00:06:23.699 --> 00:06:28.090
he did, because certainly he wasn't the only
person trying to be powerful or influential

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:29.090
in Hollywood?

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:30.090
David Maisel: Right.

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:34.690
No, it's interesting, Tim, because I ended
up being — I was employee number 400 out

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:35.780
of 400.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:42.070
I quickly, because of this secret project
and the fact that it became pretty obvious,

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:49.120
he was also considering leaving CAA to run
Universal to whoever bought it, which was

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:50.510
cataclysmic at the time.

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:54.500
I had this secret and we flew to Japan and
all that kind of stuff together.

00:06:54.500 --> 00:07:01.000
I became to his right-hand side and stayed
there pretty much through 1999, 2000, four

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:04.389
or five years.

00:07:04.389 --> 00:07:12.170
I saw what he's notorious for, which is a
tenacity, and a focus, and a work ethic that

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:20.190
would not let anything but his success happen,
which was combined with a level of street

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:30.970
smarts on how this town works and a ability
to amass leverage and perceive leverage that

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:37.690
caused his wishes to get executed one way
or the other.

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:45.449
Then, also combined with amazing use of PR
to create this mythical image of himself.

00:07:45.449 --> 00:07:53.789
Personally, I was able to watch that because
Michael is a controversial figure, that's

00:07:53.789 --> 00:08:00.820
very clear from the history, but I was able
to look and see where we were similar and

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:07.009
where I might want to take a different path
in my own career, but if it wasn't for Michael

00:08:07.009 --> 00:08:12.669
hiring me and giving me that break, I don't
think I'm in a position to do Marvel Studios

00:08:12.669 --> 00:08:14.889
ever.

00:08:14.889 --> 00:08:17.030
He helped.

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:23.250
I was naive as hell when I moved to L.A. I
was smart from a book sense, academic.

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:28.479
I had no idea about personal street smarts,
let alone Hollywood street smarts.

00:08:28.479 --> 00:08:35.219
I was somewhat creative, I felt, but I didn't
know how this town really works.

00:08:35.219 --> 00:08:41.130
Not only did I get to see Michael, Tim, I
had access to all the information at CAA,

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:42.130
all the deeds.

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:43.130
I just soaked it up.

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:44.130
Tim Ferriss: That's a big deal.

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:45.130
David Maisel: Yeah.

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:49.390
Tim Ferriss: I'm going to pick a little bit
more at the origin story, not in a critical

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:51.320
way, but in a curious way.

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:52.320
David Maisel: Sure.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:57.420
Tim Ferriss: If you have to speculate, feel
free to throw in the caveat that you're speculating.

00:08:57.420 --> 00:09:04.920
Cover of Businessweek, putting himself in
these PR positions that would create a dynamic

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:10.040
that might help him do whatever it is that
Michael wanted to do, which I believe he also

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:19.630
imparted to Marc Andreessen and Ben Horowitz
when they were founding Andreessen Horowitz,

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:25.269
a16z, and they did a masterful job in turn
of utilizing targeted PR.

00:09:25.269 --> 00:09:28.560
The reason I bring that up is he's on the
cover of Businessweek.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:35.160
I have to imagine you are not the only business
school graduate to reach out to him.

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:41.740
Even if you have to reach, what was it that
— you're probably not the only consultant

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:42.740
either —
David Maisel: Yeah.

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:44.660
Tim Ferriss: — who reached out to him.

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:49.990
Why do you think you got the extensive five-minute
interview?

00:09:49.990 --> 00:09:52.500
David Maisel: Well, there's two questions.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:56.810
There's one, why the interview and the second,
which I think you're relating to too, which

00:09:56.810 --> 00:09:58.290
is why'd I get hired, right?

00:09:58.290 --> 00:09:59.290
Tim Ferriss: Yup.

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:00.290
Totally.

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:01.320
David Maisel: The interview one, it's a good
question.

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:02.930
Nobody's ever asked me that before.

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:07.329
It wasn't like I went through three people
to get to Michael.

00:10:07.329 --> 00:10:12.240
I think I spoke to a couple other people that
same day, but Michael was on my schedule.

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:14.780
I had my time with him.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:21.160
In that five, it was no more than 10-minute
period, I do remember connecting.

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:32.170
I don't know how, but I think he saw my tenacity
that I was willing and committed to do what

00:10:32.170 --> 00:10:33.170
needed to get done.

00:10:33.170 --> 00:10:36.040
I can't —
Tim Ferriss: He saw that through stories of

00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:39.600
projects, assignments that you'd worked on
in the past —

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:41.600
David Maisel: Yeah, I can't recall —
Tim Ferriss: — or it's just a feeling?

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:44.990
David Maisel: — what he talked to me about,
but I'd gone to Duke.

00:10:44.990 --> 00:10:46.860
I was number two in my class.

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:50.720
I was on the board of trustees when I graduated
as a student, a full board member.

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:51.740
I was —
Tim Ferriss: That's wild.

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:53.339
David Maisel: — accepted right into HBS.

00:10:53.339 --> 00:10:57.370
Yeah, I had his great — I produced the Broadway
at Duke series.

00:10:57.370 --> 00:11:00.570
Not that that matters, but it was relevant
to entertainment.

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:01.570
He saw — you're right.

00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:04.750
I mean, a lot of people have great resumes.

00:11:04.750 --> 00:11:08.760
I think it was the energy together, but something
I've never told anybody is he didn't hire

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:10.540
me right away.

00:11:10.540 --> 00:11:14.470
That interview happened.

00:11:14.470 --> 00:11:18.870
I didn't hear anything back for a while, and
I started interviewing with other people.

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:24.260
I remember I had a job offer from David Stern
at the NBA, which was, wow.

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:28.930
I mean, we were just talking about the Celtics,
but this was, I had an interview with the

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:34.340
commissioner of the NBA and he hired me to
be vice president of Entertainment and I remember

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:39.660
he offered me 50,000 a year, and I was like,
"Mr. Stern, how can I live in Manhattan at

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:40.670
50,000 a year?"

00:11:40.670 --> 00:11:42.720
He was like, "Don't live in Manhattan."

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:45.640
That was his answer.

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:50.680
I actually interviewed with Marvel and got
a job offer from Marvel at the same time as

00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:54.420
head of business development when it was owned
by Ron Perelman.

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:56.920
I remember their Manhattan offices.

00:11:56.920 --> 00:12:02.860
Then I got an offer from McKinsey, which was
like triple the money, which I needed, to

00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:08.040
be a median entertainment specialist for them
worldwide, like a new position.

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:15.880
I literally accepted that offer from McKinsey,
moved to New York City, and started on a Monday.

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:18.700
I wrote a letter, an email, I forget what
it was at the time, but it must have been

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:24.029
a letter or an email to Michael and a guy
named Sandy Climan, saying, "Guys, just so

00:12:24.029 --> 00:12:28.680
you know, I accepted another job at McKinsey."

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:33.530
On Wednesday I got a phone call from Sandy
Climan who was working for Michael, saying,

00:12:33.530 --> 00:12:37.160
"Is it too late for you to join us?"

00:12:37.160 --> 00:12:39.220
I gave notice on Thursday.

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:43.130
I was at McKinsey for one week, maybe it was,
I don't know.

00:12:43.130 --> 00:12:49.380
Sandy gave me a note from Michael on top of
the letter I sent saying, "Is it too late

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:50.839
to get David?"

00:12:50.839 --> 00:12:52.279
I think it was timing.

00:12:52.279 --> 00:12:57.570
It really was the timing that pieced things
together, but I can't explain it.

00:12:57.570 --> 00:12:59.470
Tim Ferriss: A little scarcity doesn't hurt
either.

00:12:59.470 --> 00:13:00.470
David Maisel: Right.

00:13:00.470 --> 00:13:02.230
Tim Ferriss: Having a bunch of excellent offers
doesn't — competing offers doesn't hurt.

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:03.230
David Maisel: Yeah.

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:08.110
With him though, you're right, your sense
that he was at the center of attention and

00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:10.200
had his selection of things.

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:15.490
I do think in my career, when I look back
on it, I was very fortunate with some timing

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:21.019
issues, both with Michael and eventually with
Marvel.

00:13:21.019 --> 00:13:28.940
I had to create both jobs, and I'm proud of
that, but I also approached them both at the

00:13:28.940 --> 00:13:29.940
right time.

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:37.089
Tim Ferriss: I think anyone who has ever been
on this podcast who has achieved outsized

00:13:37.089 --> 00:13:45.440
success, if they're being really honest, has
to admit there is a huge factor of timing.

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:51.090
They just have to, which doesn't demean or
minimize the hard work required to translate

00:13:51.090 --> 00:13:55.780
that into amazing outcomes, but nonetheless,
it's an ingredient.

00:13:55.780 --> 00:14:00.149
Let me ask you just a few more questions about
that period.

00:14:00.149 --> 00:14:03.880
For people listening who are like, "We want
to talk about the creative stuff," we're going

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:09.250
to get to the creative stuff, but understand,
from my perspective, this is all creative

00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:10.250
stuff.

00:14:10.250 --> 00:14:11.250
David Maisel: Yeah, it is.

00:14:11.250 --> 00:14:15.339
Tim Ferriss: I'll give you a sense of what
I mean, for those people who don't have context,

00:14:15.339 --> 00:14:24.430
McKinsey, BCG, Boston Consulting Group, these
are, for many graduates of good schools, coveted

00:14:24.430 --> 00:14:26.639
jobs, highly competitive jobs.

00:14:26.639 --> 00:14:33.870
They're also firms within which if you play
your cards right and you keep your head down,

00:14:33.870 --> 00:14:43.339
you can make a lot of money and it takes time
but that is one potential option.

00:14:43.339 --> 00:14:51.180
It would seem to be more predictable, perhaps
more perceived as more secure, stable than

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:53.040
some of these options you were looking at.

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:54.040
David Maisel: Yup.

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:59.060
Tim Ferriss: What led you — and a lot of
people go into consulting with or investment

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:04.029
banking for that matter, and they say, "I'm
going to do this just for a few years, get

00:15:04.029 --> 00:15:08.600
exposure to a broad array of industries, and
then I'm going to forge out on my own and

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:09.600
do something entrepreneurial."

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:12.350
That doesn't always happen, as you know.

00:15:12.350 --> 00:15:13.430
David Maisel: Yup.

00:15:13.430 --> 00:15:20.420
Tim Ferriss: What contributed to you at that
time deciding to branch, ultimately recognizing,

00:15:20.420 --> 00:15:25.470
you did accept the job at McKinsey, but then
ended up with Michael, but nonetheless, to

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:27.449
explore options outside of consulting?

00:15:27.449 --> 00:15:30.770
David Maisel: No, it was very personal.

00:15:30.770 --> 00:15:36.490
I wish I could say I had realized this on
my own, but my sister, older sister passed

00:15:36.490 --> 00:15:43.100
away and at young age, and I saw impermanence,
and I saw the fragility of life for the first

00:15:43.100 --> 00:15:45.500
time.

00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:46.810
It did affect me.

00:15:46.810 --> 00:15:54.450
I knew I wasn't completely happy not being
creative, not at least trying to be out in

00:15:54.450 --> 00:16:01.220
the middle of the action and in the arena
and quit BCG soon after that.

00:16:01.220 --> 00:16:05.480
When I wrote Michael that letter, I was back
home living with my mom, which wasn't — now,

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:06.720
that's popular to do.

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:10.079
Back then, it really wasn't.

00:16:10.079 --> 00:16:13.980
I ended up taking the McKinsey thing because
I had run out of money that I had saved from

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:14.980
BCG.

00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:15.980
I was forced to take something.

00:16:15.980 --> 00:16:18.310
I think I tried to learn poker at the time.

00:16:18.310 --> 00:16:23.560
I was losing some money at the games that
were going on.

00:16:23.560 --> 00:16:32.230
It was a gift for my sister, frankly, to make
me realize that we should try to live to minimize

00:16:32.230 --> 00:16:33.459
regrets, if possible.

00:16:33.459 --> 00:16:36.550
Tim Ferriss: I'm so sorry to hear that.

00:16:36.550 --> 00:16:41.380
I imagine that must have been really challenging
for your whole family and for you.

00:16:41.380 --> 00:16:42.380
David Maisel: Yeah.

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:48.280
It was really a beautiful gift though, because
I think I might not have been, have the backbone

00:16:48.280 --> 00:16:52.940
to, like you say, a lot of people talk about
branching out, but you can get really secure

00:16:52.940 --> 00:16:57.160
at those places.

00:16:57.160 --> 00:16:58.160
I was headstrong.

00:16:58.160 --> 00:17:02.370
In fact, when Michael offered me the job,
he said, "What are you making at McKinsey?"

00:17:02.370 --> 00:17:07.250
I told him and he said, "We'll match that,
but there's no guarantee, and I can fire you

00:17:07.250 --> 00:17:09.439
tomorrow."

00:17:09.439 --> 00:17:10.660
It was a gamble.

00:17:10.660 --> 00:17:11.820
It was definitely a gamble.

00:17:11.820 --> 00:17:15.839
Tim Ferriss: "How am I supposed to live in
Manhattan?"

00:17:15.839 --> 00:17:16.839
"Don't live in Manhattan," and it's —
David Maisel: Yeah.

00:17:16.839 --> 00:17:20.839
Especially from the commissioner who had just
signed this huge deal with the owners.

00:17:20.839 --> 00:17:27.650
It's like, "Come on, you're not shy for money
at the NBA."

00:17:27.650 --> 00:17:30.320
Tim Ferriss: It's kind of an amazing answer,
though.

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:32.400
You're like, "I can't really…"

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:34.900
Hard to refute, in a sense.

00:17:34.900 --> 00:17:43.190
Let's come back to something that you've mentioned
in passing, but the creative interests that

00:17:43.190 --> 00:17:53.100
you turned off in favor of developing or honing
in on your rational, analytical mind.

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:54.370
What were those interests?

00:17:54.370 --> 00:17:59.910
David Maisel: It was, primarily when I grew
up, acting in theater and I was an actor in

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:06.170
high school and love live theater, and luckily
my career brought me to have the chance to

00:18:06.170 --> 00:18:10.020
produce multiple shows, which I've really
enjoyed.

00:18:10.020 --> 00:18:12.020
I also just love culture and entertainment.

00:18:12.020 --> 00:18:14.260
Star Wars was a huge impact.

00:18:14.260 --> 00:18:22.340
The idea of crafting mega cultural movies,
to me, as a creative producer, as a business

00:18:22.340 --> 00:18:25.580
producer combined, was really exciting.

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:31.220
It was really show business in general.

00:18:31.220 --> 00:18:33.770
I knew my acting skills were not that great.

00:18:33.770 --> 00:18:39.630
They're pretty bad, but the idea of being
a creative producer on Broadway or film, that's

00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:41.080
what seemed the most exciting to me.

00:18:41.080 --> 00:18:47.280
Tim Ferriss: I want to quote someone and then
get —

00:18:47.280 --> 00:18:48.280
David Maisel: Sure.

00:18:48.280 --> 00:18:49.280
Tim Ferriss: — a little insight from you.

00:18:49.280 --> 00:18:54.380
This is a quote from, I believe, I'm getting
the pronunciation right, Kevin Huvane —

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:55.380
David Maisel: Yeah.

00:18:55.380 --> 00:19:01.409
Tim Ferriss: — as quoted in the New York
Times and the quote is, "David has..." this

00:19:01.409 --> 00:19:07.590
is in sort of brackets, but, "...encyclopedic
knowledge of comic books and treats them like

00:19:07.590 --> 00:19:11.720
real art with savant-like ability to take
out characters that appeal to him and make

00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:13.840
them really relevant."

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:16.350
When did your relationship with comic books
begin?

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:18.030
David Maisel: Early.

00:19:18.030 --> 00:19:23.360
I mean, it was probably a lot of us, something
I loved as a kid, probably when I started

00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:30.330
reading, five or six, and Marvel specifically,
but overall comic books and Iron Man specifically,

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:32.250
but a lot of characters.

00:19:32.250 --> 00:19:36.740
I'm excited to see the new Flash movie that's
coming out.

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:42.300
It was something, I remember my mom, I used
to joke with her recently before she passed

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:49.100
that she would tell me to stop reading my
comic books and focus on schoolwork.

00:19:49.100 --> 00:19:50.850
It turned out to be my career.

00:19:50.850 --> 00:19:53.190
It was pretty ironic.

00:19:53.190 --> 00:20:01.010
Kevin, who gave that quote, I remember being
touched by that because when I joined CAA,

00:20:01.010 --> 00:20:08.559
back to Michael, Kevin Huvane, and Bryan Lourd
and Richard Lovett, the three people who are

00:20:08.559 --> 00:20:13.880
predominantly running CAA today and took over
the firm when Michael left and are arguably

00:20:13.880 --> 00:20:19.460
some of the — if not the — most powerful
agents in town.

00:20:19.460 --> 00:20:27.929
We sort of grew up together in the mid-'90s
working for Michael, and it was an unexpected

00:20:27.929 --> 00:20:39.190
quote when I saw that, but it was nice to
hear his kudos coming from his position.

00:20:39.190 --> 00:20:40.600
I guess we did do some deals.

00:20:40.600 --> 00:20:47.289
Natalie Portman was his client, who was in
Thor and many others, as well from CAA.

00:20:47.289 --> 00:20:49.980
Comics were always an interest.

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:55.720
Marvel specifically, Tim, was a fascination
with me.

00:20:55.720 --> 00:20:59.700
Even before I got that job offer when I was
talking to Michael, when I was at business

00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:04.790
school over beers, some of my friends and
I were like, "We need to leverage buyout Marvel

00:21:04.790 --> 00:21:07.299
from this vitamin company that owned it."

00:21:07.299 --> 00:21:11.390
It was completely tilting at a windmill and
nothing came from it.

00:21:11.390 --> 00:21:15.909
Tim Ferriss: What a fun windmill to tilt at
though, while you're slamming back beers at

00:21:15.909 --> 00:21:16.909
HBS.

00:21:16.909 --> 00:21:17.909
That's great.

00:21:17.909 --> 00:21:18.909
David Maisel: Exactly.

00:21:18.909 --> 00:21:23.840
Tim Ferriss: Foreshadowing, I think that's
what they might call that.

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:32.270
Before we get to Marvel, what are other lessons
that you learned either from Michael or just

00:21:32.270 --> 00:21:35.250
during that chapter of your Hollywood experience?

00:21:35.250 --> 00:21:38.240
I would love —
David Maisel: I think —

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:45.690
Tim Ferriss: — to just know what kind of
principles or lessons you ended up absorbing,

00:21:45.690 --> 00:21:49.450
best practices, anything that comes to mind.

00:21:49.450 --> 00:21:54.309
David Maisel: Tim, I think there's a couple
that I'll point out, when was Michael really

00:21:54.309 --> 00:22:00.919
taught me how to work with the creatives of
this town, because he put me in front of them.

00:22:00.919 --> 00:22:06.660
I remember, it might have been the first or
second time I was ever with him at an industry

00:22:06.660 --> 00:22:08.760
function.

00:22:08.760 --> 00:22:13.900
He always, and I'll always give him credit
for this, whether he said it or not, but often

00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:19.140
many times he said something like, "Hey, here's
a lesson here."

00:22:19.140 --> 00:22:21.240
Tom Cruise was there.

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:26.220
He introduced me to Tom Cruise, and then he
said, "We'll be right back."

00:22:26.220 --> 00:22:31.430
He took me to the bar and Michael ordered
drinks for Tom and whoever he was with and

00:22:31.430 --> 00:22:37.169
brought the drinks back for Tom and whoever
was around him at the time, whether it's his

00:22:37.169 --> 00:22:41.779
assistant or girlfriend at the time, I don't
know.

00:22:41.779 --> 00:22:46.820
He mentioned to me, "Just remember, we're
always in the service business.

00:22:46.820 --> 00:22:49.550
Remember who our customers are."

00:22:49.550 --> 00:22:54.440
The fact he did that personally and didn't
ask me to go do it, or somebody else, was

00:22:54.440 --> 00:23:00.270
like, "Whoa, this guy is at a different level."

00:23:00.270 --> 00:23:06.320
I remember the second day I was there, he
brought me into a meeting with Bill Gates

00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:13.870
at the time, who was, and they talked about
creating a competitor to AOL, and I saw how

00:23:13.870 --> 00:23:18.770
he dealt with the tech and which, as you hinted,
became a big part of his life later on.

00:23:18.770 --> 00:23:20.880
He always was on the cutting edge of technology.

00:23:20.880 --> 00:23:26.799
He opened my eyes to keeping an eye out to
what's going on with tech and entertainment

00:23:26.799 --> 00:23:31.860
and how those two industries are still trying
to figure out how to work together.

00:23:31.860 --> 00:23:34.720
Michael was sort of ahead of the time on that.

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:43.309
Then, I guess, third was all of us have to
figure out our personal style and approach.

00:23:43.309 --> 00:23:50.040
I saw how there's choices to get made on how
you sort of learn about Hollywood and progress

00:23:50.040 --> 00:23:51.520
through Hollywood.

00:23:51.520 --> 00:24:01.130
That, to me, was eye-opening because I also
realized I wasn't very good at that game of

00:24:01.130 --> 00:24:09.010
using leverage or the politics of what needs
to get done in any entity, but this time specifically,

00:24:09.010 --> 00:24:10.010
Hollywood.

00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:11.420
It was an eye-opening lesson for me.

00:24:11.420 --> 00:24:18.539
Tim Ferriss: I asked a professor of mine at
one point what it was like working in DC because

00:24:18.539 --> 00:24:22.630
he ended up being a congressman and spending
a bunch of time in politics.

00:24:22.630 --> 00:24:26.320
He said, "It's like Hollywood, but with less
attractive people."

00:24:26.320 --> 00:24:33.380
I always thought that was hilarious and probably
accurate on some level.

00:24:33.380 --> 00:24:37.250
You have said before, this is from The Financial
Times, that to be really successful in Hollywood,

00:24:37.250 --> 00:24:42.650
and you already alluded to some of this, one
needs to be very good at business fundamentals,

00:24:42.650 --> 00:24:46.789
managing the creative process and street smarts
to understand the town.

00:24:46.789 --> 00:24:55.990
Could you give an example of each of those,
perhaps case studies, times when you absorbed

00:24:55.990 --> 00:24:59.929
lessons related to any of those, the business
fundamentals, managing the creative process

00:24:59.929 --> 00:25:01.980
and street smarts to understand the town?

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:08.281
David Maisel: The first one, business fundamentals,
it became pretty clear to me that that was

00:25:08.281 --> 00:25:12.429
an area that Hollywood was not very focused
on.

00:25:12.429 --> 00:25:17.180
To this day, you probably can make the same
case if you look at some of these enterprise

00:25:17.180 --> 00:25:23.600
values of the studios, but the idea of shareholder
value, the idea of the what creates a Disney

00:25:23.600 --> 00:25:29.059
to be worth more money, or Universal, how
do you value a business?

00:25:29.059 --> 00:25:34.980
That — the idea of value was foreign to
people.

00:25:34.980 --> 00:25:41.950
To his credit, I remember Bryan Lourd, who
is running CAA now and is one of the most

00:25:41.950 --> 00:25:48.049
amazing people in this town, and I say that
in all respects, but I remember him pulling

00:25:48.049 --> 00:25:55.980
me aside back in 1995 and asking me to tutor
him on financial statements and shareholder

00:25:55.980 --> 00:25:58.150
value and all those types of things.

00:25:58.150 --> 00:26:01.750
Now, he's running this huge agency, you know
what I mean?

00:26:01.750 --> 00:26:08.010
He's also saw the same thing and was wise
enough to realize it was something that he

00:26:08.010 --> 00:26:11.559
could use to his advantage once he learned
it.

00:26:11.559 --> 00:26:19.320
That actually becomes really important, because
later on, I realized the market here was inefficient

00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:24.429
and that the fact I was able to do what we'll
talk about later with Marvel should never

00:26:24.429 --> 00:26:26.760
have been able to happen if it was efficient,
right?

00:26:26.760 --> 00:26:27.760
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:26:27.760 --> 00:26:31.510
David Maisel: Then, number two, with the creative
process, God, there's so many things there,

00:26:31.510 --> 00:26:38.530
but everything like that Tom Cruise story,
which is understanding them.

00:26:38.530 --> 00:26:43.549
Michael also talked about always the people
around the star that it was so important to

00:26:43.549 --> 00:26:45.140
have a great relationship with them.

00:26:45.140 --> 00:26:46.140
Tim Ferriss: That's smart.

00:26:46.140 --> 00:26:53.370
David Maisel: He exposed me to what a star
like that is used to in Hollywood.

00:26:53.370 --> 00:26:58.580
Understanding that they have other people
around them that will be maybe whispering

00:26:58.580 --> 00:27:00.480
in their ears, negative things.

00:27:00.480 --> 00:27:03.429
You have to play defense and offense.

00:27:03.429 --> 00:27:08.320
I was so fortunate, Michael put me in front
of so many people.

00:27:08.320 --> 00:27:15.740
I remember him sending me to Warren Beatty's
house, just this icon and still is, to talk

00:27:15.740 --> 00:27:18.029
about the stock market.

00:27:18.029 --> 00:27:24.570
I only say this because it just shows you
how forward-thinking Michael was.

00:27:24.570 --> 00:27:28.500
He gave me a chance to interact with this
megastar on a personal basis.

00:27:28.500 --> 00:27:32.929
I got to training of —
Tim Ferriss: Now, did Warren just reach out

00:27:32.929 --> 00:27:35.770
to Michael and say, "I want to learn about
the stock market.

00:27:35.770 --> 00:27:36.770
Find somebody who'll help me?"

00:27:36.770 --> 00:27:37.870
David Maisel: I think it was the opposite.

00:27:37.870 --> 00:27:43.409
I think what I learned when I got there, because
I remember I sat with him for five minutes,

00:27:43.409 --> 00:27:46.980
awestruck, and then he basically looked at
me and said, "Hey, kid, there's no way I'm

00:27:46.980 --> 00:27:47.980
ever going to learn this.

00:27:47.980 --> 00:27:49.440
I've had too much fun in my life.

00:27:49.440 --> 00:27:51.390
I'm not going to start focusing on the stock
market."

00:27:51.390 --> 00:28:00.799
Now, he might have used a different word than
fun with an F. It definitely was Michael tutoring

00:28:00.799 --> 00:28:01.799
his clients.

00:28:01.799 --> 00:28:06.860
I think Magic Johnson has been — publicly
talks about how Michael would get him a Businessweek

00:28:06.860 --> 00:28:09.450
subscription and things like that —
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

00:28:09.450 --> 00:28:10.840
David Maisel: — while he was a basketball
player.

00:28:10.840 --> 00:28:11.940
Tim Ferriss: Magic has run with that.

00:28:11.940 --> 00:28:13.429
He's been so —
David Maisel: Exactly.

00:28:13.429 --> 00:28:14.890
Tim Ferriss: — so good at executing.

00:28:14.890 --> 00:28:15.890
Wow.

00:28:15.890 --> 00:28:16.890
David Maisel: Yeah.

00:28:16.890 --> 00:28:17.890
Tim Ferriss: That's wild.

00:28:17.890 --> 00:28:24.880
David Maisel: The third, street smarts, wow,
I mean, that one, I just realized how small

00:28:24.880 --> 00:28:26.570
this town is, Tim.

00:28:26.570 --> 00:28:30.100
It really is.

00:28:30.100 --> 00:28:34.870
It's a small group of people and still is.

00:28:34.870 --> 00:28:42.480
Understanding what people want and how you
can make a deal by figuring out the magic

00:28:42.480 --> 00:28:48.620
of delivering what they want by getting what
you want back and what people's, the street

00:28:48.620 --> 00:28:52.450
smarts they'd say, wow, I've never talked
about this publicly, is to know that it's

00:28:52.450 --> 00:28:53.789
what is their motivation.

00:28:53.789 --> 00:28:54.990
Are they going for their annual bonus?

00:28:54.990 --> 00:28:57.470
Are they going for the best seat in the restaurant?

00:28:57.470 --> 00:28:59.110
Do they care about their stock options?

00:28:59.110 --> 00:29:00.210
They care about their prestige?

00:29:00.210 --> 00:29:03.090
Do they care about credit?

00:29:03.090 --> 00:29:08.300
Many different people here are motivated by
other things.

00:29:08.300 --> 00:29:11.290
Sometimes it was good, street smart wise to
figure that out.

00:29:11.290 --> 00:29:13.840
It helped me the rest of my career.

00:29:13.840 --> 00:29:19.539
Tim Ferriss: If someone is listening and they
say, "At some point I would like to set foot

00:29:19.539 --> 00:29:25.039
into what we would broadly call entertainment,"
and they suspect they're going to end up in

00:29:25.039 --> 00:29:31.620
Hollywood or L.A. or at least interacting
with groups from those places.

00:29:31.620 --> 00:29:34.350
They're not going to have Michael Ovitz.

00:29:34.350 --> 00:29:35.350
David Maisel: Yes.

00:29:35.350 --> 00:29:36.990
Tim Ferriss: They may not have a direct mentor.

00:29:36.990 --> 00:29:45.470
Are there any books or resources or docs or
they could just be scripted movies too, that

00:29:45.470 --> 00:29:49.620
you would encourage people to watch to help
them with those buckets.

00:29:49.620 --> 00:29:53.590
David Maisel: I'm thinking about that off
the top of my head.

00:29:53.590 --> 00:29:59.750
There are biographies of Michael, of David
Geffen, of industry figures like that.

00:29:59.750 --> 00:30:05.340
I think those are useful to understand the
history of the town.

00:30:05.340 --> 00:30:11.000
I also am surprised, we used to have paper
trades, The Hollywood Reporter and Variety,

00:30:11.000 --> 00:30:17.690
they're now all online, but how many times
I interview young people that want to come

00:30:17.690 --> 00:30:21.519
into Hollywood and they're not really up-to-date
even on the current stuff that's happening,

00:30:21.519 --> 00:30:22.519
right?

00:30:22.519 --> 00:30:23.519
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:30:23.519 --> 00:30:25.820
David Maisel: There's so much information
available that wasn't available to me at the

00:30:25.820 --> 00:30:26.820
time.

00:30:26.820 --> 00:30:34.640
Diving into history, through articles and
through biographies, and just keeping up every

00:30:34.640 --> 00:30:39.170
day with what the latest news in Hollywood
is.

00:30:39.170 --> 00:30:43.970
Even just those two things together, if you
don't have a mentor, you'll get the benefit

00:30:43.970 --> 00:30:46.169
of a lot of learning, like in most areas of
life.

00:30:46.169 --> 00:30:47.169
Yeah.

00:30:47.169 --> 00:30:48.169
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, totally.

00:30:48.169 --> 00:30:53.789
And I'll reinforce a point that might not
be the sexy piece for a lot of folks, but

00:30:53.789 --> 00:31:00.750
the business fundamentals, it's if you want
to, as you have done and we're going to segue

00:31:00.750 --> 00:31:10.241
shortly, capitalize on inefficient markets
or asymmetric upside opportunities, having

00:31:10.241 --> 00:31:16.610
a grasp of just a handful of fundamentals
is so incredibly valuable.

00:31:16.610 --> 00:31:22.139
Another example of that would be Bill Gurley,
who's a spectacularly successful venture capitalist

00:31:22.139 --> 00:31:28.620
and famous mostly — well, I shouldn't say
mostly, but he's associated with Benchmark.

00:31:28.620 --> 00:31:35.299
And he came from Wall Street, having developed
these different valuation models or at least

00:31:35.299 --> 00:31:42.789
used all sorts of tools for establishing value,
and got to tech in Silicon Valley and saw

00:31:42.789 --> 00:31:43.789
what you saw in Hollywood.

00:31:43.789 --> 00:31:48.850
It's like, "Wow, people really aren't tracking
this stuff."

00:31:48.850 --> 00:31:57.669
And being able to sort of retrofit those to
this new environment allowed him and his partners

00:31:57.669 --> 00:31:59.539
to find some incredible opportunities.

00:31:59.539 --> 00:32:00.630
David Maisel: Exactly.

00:32:00.630 --> 00:32:05.040
Tim Ferriss: So let's talk about Marvel.

00:32:05.040 --> 00:32:07.690
How does Marvel enter the picture?

00:32:07.690 --> 00:32:12.289
David Maisel: Well, what happened, and it
relates to CAA and Michael, is Michael did

00:32:12.289 --> 00:32:14.809
not take the job with Universal.

00:32:14.809 --> 00:32:17.980
We sold it to Seagram, to Edgar Bronfman.

00:32:17.980 --> 00:32:26.010
And famously, Michael was supposed to run
it and ended up not getting the job, and it

00:32:26.010 --> 00:32:29.151
was given to Ron Meyer, who was Michael's
partner at CAA.

00:32:29.151 --> 00:32:34.700
It was somewhat Shakespearean because Michael
and Ron were so close.

00:32:34.700 --> 00:32:40.970
And perhaps — the papers say Michael might
have over-asked for what he wanted from Edgar

00:32:40.970 --> 00:32:41.970
Bronfman.

00:32:41.970 --> 00:32:47.200
But he hired Ron Meyer, who ended up spending
20 or 25 years, or 20 years, running Universal

00:32:47.200 --> 00:32:48.419
Studios.

00:32:48.419 --> 00:32:52.990
Everyone thought then that Michael was going
to stay at CAA, but then he decided to go

00:32:52.990 --> 00:32:59.360
to Disney and be president of the Walt Disney
Company, with his friend Michael Eisner, who

00:32:59.360 --> 00:33:00.820
was CEO.

00:33:00.820 --> 00:33:03.419
That was cataclysmic in Hollywood.

00:33:03.419 --> 00:33:12.559
I remember walking to a meeting that Michael
called early in the morning to announce this.

00:33:12.559 --> 00:33:19.399
And then later that day, he called me to his
office and he invited me to join him at Disney.

00:33:19.399 --> 00:33:24.910
I had a job offer from Ron Meyer to go to
Universal as well, and the third option was

00:33:24.910 --> 00:33:29.529
to stay with the new owners of CAA, Kevin
and Ryan and others.

00:33:29.529 --> 00:33:30.889
But I loved Disney.

00:33:30.889 --> 00:33:33.110
I grew up loving the Disney parks.

00:33:33.110 --> 00:33:37.590
We haven't talked about this, but when I graduated
from business school, the job offer I turned

00:33:37.590 --> 00:33:43.120
down when I went to BCG was for the Walt Disney
Company, for strategic planning.

00:33:43.120 --> 00:33:49.750
Tom Staggs, who ended up almost becoming CEO,
was in the waiting room with me at the time.

00:33:49.750 --> 00:33:54.799
So I always had this interest in the Walt
Disney Company, and Michael was my guy, right?

00:33:54.799 --> 00:33:59.049
And here's my idol now asking me to join him
in his new chapter.

00:33:59.049 --> 00:34:05.070
So that turned out to be critical because
I then got to spend a couple years at Disney,

00:34:05.070 --> 00:34:07.669
where it was like your dad was the president,
right?

00:34:07.669 --> 00:34:10.869
So you had open access to everything.

00:34:10.869 --> 00:34:16.111
Like I did at CAA, now I'm in a studio and
I'm seeing from a studio perspective all the

00:34:16.111 --> 00:34:19.649
games that are played and how they view the
world.

00:34:19.649 --> 00:34:23.879
And I was put in strategic planning and corporate
development, which was a powerhouse group

00:34:23.879 --> 00:34:24.879
at Disney.

00:34:24.879 --> 00:34:26.800
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, you should explain what
corporate development does.

00:34:26.800 --> 00:34:27.800
David Maisel: Yeah.

00:34:27.800 --> 00:34:31.010
Tim Ferriss: Kind of like, you couldn't script
this movie better, in a way.

00:34:31.010 --> 00:34:37.119
David Maisel: Corporate development at Disney
was — Michael Eisner had this group of people

00:34:37.119 --> 00:34:44.369
that controlled strategy and every major move
under him, and they would work with division

00:34:44.369 --> 00:34:45.369
heads.

00:34:45.369 --> 00:34:51.429
Essentially, it was his way to centralize
major decisions at Disney and sort of control

00:34:51.429 --> 00:34:52.990
those decisions.

00:34:52.990 --> 00:34:59.290
I was put in an office next to Kevin Mayer,
who ended up almost becoming CEO, Tom Staggs,

00:34:59.290 --> 00:35:00.630
who I mentioned.

00:35:00.630 --> 00:35:06.350
So we would go to meetings with Michael Eisner
and Michael Ovitz, and I was Michael Ovitz's

00:35:06.350 --> 00:35:09.780
guy, they were Michael Eisner's guys, and
we'd come back and we'd all get our phone

00:35:09.780 --> 00:35:12.050
calls and it was just a crazy fun time.

00:35:12.050 --> 00:35:19.869
They just bought ABC, and I remember Michael
Eisner saying, and Ovitz, "David, what are

00:35:19.869 --> 00:35:20.869
you interested in?"

00:35:20.869 --> 00:35:23.890
And I said, "Well, sports, and ESPN is sort
of cool.

00:35:23.890 --> 00:35:26.140
I'll help out ABC."

00:35:26.140 --> 00:35:32.280
And because of that, I was the liaison on
corporate development with Bob Iger, who was

00:35:32.280 --> 00:35:38.210
the president of that division at the time,
and Steve Burke, who was his number two guy,

00:35:38.210 --> 00:35:41.730
who became the head of Universal, Comcast
later on.

00:35:41.730 --> 00:35:47.950
So I was so fortunate to be exposed to so
much in a two-year period, Tim — good and

00:35:47.950 --> 00:35:52.580
bad — about what happens at a studio like
Disney.

00:35:52.580 --> 00:35:57.230
That's one of the things my job was, was to
recommend acquisitions.

00:35:57.230 --> 00:36:01.400
And the three thread with Marvel, I recommended
— I don't even think this is public, so

00:36:01.400 --> 00:36:02.579
this is breaking news.

00:36:02.579 --> 00:36:08.089
I recommended that Disney buy Marvel, back
in like '96, '97, right?

00:36:08.089 --> 00:36:12.000
At the time, it was no more than a hundred
million dollars.

00:36:12.000 --> 00:36:17.290
And I remember getting kicked out of the room,
basically, because one of the first things

00:36:17.290 --> 00:36:22.050
that Michael Eisner and the other people in
the room saw was that there was no theme park

00:36:22.050 --> 00:36:27.530
rights available east of the Mississippi River,
because that was a deal that Ron Perelman

00:36:27.530 --> 00:36:29.160
had given to Universal.

00:36:29.160 --> 00:36:37.110
And because of that, I sort of was not gently
told that this was a stupid idea.

00:36:37.110 --> 00:36:38.700
Thankfully —
Tim Ferriss: What year was this roughly?

00:36:38.700 --> 00:36:39.960
David Maisel: '96, '97.

00:36:39.960 --> 00:36:41.220
Tim Ferriss: '96, '97.

00:36:41.220 --> 00:36:47.910
Yeah, I'm also thinking of the kind of state
of the art, at the time, CGI, what was possible,

00:36:47.910 --> 00:36:49.250
what was not possible.

00:36:49.250 --> 00:36:55.520
In your mind, what was the selling or value
proposition at that time —

00:36:55.520 --> 00:36:56.520
David Maisel: For Marvel.

00:36:56.520 --> 00:36:57.730
Tim Ferriss: — for the recommendation?

00:36:57.730 --> 00:37:01.609
What were the bullet points in the recommendation
for that acquisition?

00:37:01.609 --> 00:37:09.690
David Maisel: The same as what I told Bob
Iger 14 years later when I actually sold him

00:37:09.690 --> 00:37:11.650
Marvel for, on paper, four billion.

00:37:11.650 --> 00:37:15.349
Tim Ferriss: You're like, "Let me just pull
this up from 14 years ago."

00:37:15.349 --> 00:37:18.550
David Maisel: No, but to Bob's credit, Bob
wasn't the decision-maker in that earlier

00:37:18.550 --> 00:37:19.550
meeting.

00:37:19.550 --> 00:37:20.550
Tim Ferriss: Right, right.

00:37:20.550 --> 00:37:23.190
David Maisel: He probably would not have even
been in the room because he was running ABC

00:37:23.190 --> 00:37:24.190
at the time.

00:37:24.190 --> 00:37:26.390
But he remembered that and I remembered it.

00:37:26.390 --> 00:37:32.630
Okay, so separate from the movies that Disney
is very good at thinking about their customer,

00:37:32.630 --> 00:37:36.190
and they're very strong with all of us as
babies, right?

00:37:36.190 --> 00:37:37.430
And we all love Disney.

00:37:37.430 --> 00:37:41.550
And then normally, guys start not liking Disney
when they're early teenagers, right?

00:37:41.550 --> 00:37:44.480
It's not cool anymore.

00:37:44.480 --> 00:37:51.980
Traditionally, that happens as well with females
at a later age, but at some point, you lose

00:37:51.980 --> 00:37:56.030
interest in what you loved as a kid, and then
it comes back when you're a young parent,

00:37:56.030 --> 00:37:58.089
right, and you have your own kids.

00:37:58.089 --> 00:38:03.130
So there was a huge gap, especially with guys
between age 12 and age whenever they have

00:38:03.130 --> 00:38:07.869
kids that Marvel was very strong with, right?

00:38:07.869 --> 00:38:09.720
So it just mapped on that.

00:38:09.720 --> 00:38:13.650
I don't even know if I was able to even get
to that point in this meeting, but it mapped

00:38:13.650 --> 00:38:21.280
on that in that hole tremendously well, and
that was the driver at the time.

00:38:21.280 --> 00:38:26.829
And then obviously, all the stuff you could
do with the Marvel characters, and I was starting

00:38:26.829 --> 00:38:32.200
to dream then about Marvel films in a bigger
way.

00:38:32.200 --> 00:38:35.859
But that idea came to me actually later, a
few years later.

00:38:35.859 --> 00:38:39.710
So it was driven more by the gap in the lifetime
cycle of a Disney customer.

00:38:39.710 --> 00:38:41.000
Tim Ferriss: That makes sense.

00:38:41.000 --> 00:38:45.850
So you make the pitch, you are very unceremoniously
ejected from the meeting.

00:38:45.850 --> 00:38:46.900
David Maisel: Yes.

00:38:46.900 --> 00:38:48.710
Which happened to me a lot, by the way.

00:38:48.710 --> 00:38:50.270
It happened many times.

00:38:50.270 --> 00:38:53.660
Tim Ferriss: And at that point, they're like,
"Yeah, great idea, kid.

00:38:53.660 --> 00:38:55.050
Not for right now, not for here."

00:38:55.050 --> 00:38:56.780
And how does Marvel —
David Maisel: I'm not sure they said, "Great

00:38:56.780 --> 00:38:57.780
idea."

00:38:57.780 --> 00:38:58.780
Yeah.

00:38:58.780 --> 00:38:59.780
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, right.

00:38:59.780 --> 00:39:02.400
So how does Marvel then boomerang and come
back to you?

00:39:02.400 --> 00:39:12.369
David Maisel: Yeah, so Michael got fired,
by his best friend, from Disney in '97, '98.

00:39:12.369 --> 00:39:19.480
And there, to make a long story short, he
again asked me to join him in this next venture

00:39:19.480 --> 00:39:22.800
of his career.

00:39:22.800 --> 00:39:27.119
That lasted a couple years, during which he
bought a Broadway company and I was able to

00:39:27.119 --> 00:39:34.520
produce a show called Fosse and exercise my
first direct creative project, this Broadway

00:39:34.520 --> 00:39:39.599
show, which did very well and we won the Tony
Award for best musical.

00:39:39.599 --> 00:39:46.290
So it gave me some confidence, which is relevant
for what happens with Marvel later on.

00:39:46.290 --> 00:39:50.460
And then I chased some internet money from
'99 to 2001.

00:39:50.460 --> 00:39:54.560
A very famous investment banker, very well
known in Hollywood named Joe Ravitch, who

00:39:54.560 --> 00:39:58.930
was at Goldman Sachs at the time, set me up
with a John Malone company, and I lived in

00:39:58.930 --> 00:40:05.640
London and Amsterdam for two years, which
was great and also scary because the dotcom

00:40:05.640 --> 00:40:10.280
crash happened in the early 2000s.

00:40:10.280 --> 00:40:16.440
And then I came back to L.A. and I was ready
to get back into traditional business.

00:40:16.440 --> 00:40:23.130
I went to work with Ari Emanuel and Patrick
Whitesell, who was a guy, a great agent, who

00:40:23.130 --> 00:40:27.260
had an office next to me at CAA, and they
had just started Endeavor, what is now William

00:40:27.260 --> 00:40:29.619
Morris Endeavor, this huge company.

00:40:29.619 --> 00:40:33.490
And they offered me my first real payday to
work for them for two years.

00:40:33.490 --> 00:40:38.780
The reason that's relevant is, besides Ari
and Patrick being amazing guys and shows you

00:40:38.780 --> 00:40:44.380
how small this town is and they still run
that company, I knew I never wanted to be

00:40:44.380 --> 00:40:46.710
an agent long term.

00:40:46.710 --> 00:40:49.560
So when that two years started coming —
Tim Ferriss: Why is that?

00:40:49.560 --> 00:40:51.520
David Maisel: It wasn't intriguing to me.

00:40:51.520 --> 00:40:56.160
In an agency, like in a consulting firm or
a law firm, there was a role for someone like

00:40:56.160 --> 00:41:02.070
me that was a wild card for a period of time,
but the real power and money came if you represented

00:41:02.070 --> 00:41:07.240
clients and you could show that you're bringing
in direct revenues, and that wasn't what I

00:41:07.240 --> 00:41:08.290
did.

00:41:08.290 --> 00:41:16.050
And I also had my own entrepreneurial desires,
and it was in 2003 when I knew I had a couple

00:41:16.050 --> 00:41:23.859
months left to go with Endeavor, that I sat
down for a weekend and figured out what has

00:41:23.859 --> 00:41:27.500
become Marvel Studios and started that path
in my life.

00:41:27.500 --> 00:41:29.080
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:41:29.080 --> 00:41:34.050
I'm going to inflict something horrible on
my audience, but I can't help myself.

00:41:34.050 --> 00:41:38.230
So this is going to be one of those dun dun
dun, like cliffhangers on page 22 of a comic

00:41:38.230 --> 00:41:39.400
book.

00:41:39.400 --> 00:41:41.710
So Marvel, we're going to come back to Marvel.

00:41:41.710 --> 00:41:50.260
But I have to very selfishly ask you about
your side path into Broadway and theater.

00:41:50.260 --> 00:41:51.260
David Maisel: Sure.

00:41:51.260 --> 00:41:55.760
Tim Ferriss: I say selfish because this, I
don't think, is known.

00:41:55.760 --> 00:41:57.650
I don't think I've mentioned this anywhere.

00:41:57.650 --> 00:42:04.480
So this is a first also that I have, over
the last few years, become one of the larger

00:42:04.480 --> 00:42:11.010
investors in a theater production, and it's
the first time I've ever become involved with

00:42:11.010 --> 00:42:12.610
that.

00:42:12.610 --> 00:42:17.880
They did a run in Pennsylvania, their first
commercial run, and now they're going to be

00:42:17.880 --> 00:42:23.090
opening a much larger production in Chicago
in the next few weeks, in fact, as of the

00:42:23.090 --> 00:42:24.090
time of this recording.

00:42:24.090 --> 00:42:26.470
For people who want to check it out, personalitymusical.com.

00:42:26.470 --> 00:42:29.060
That's the story of Lloyd Price.

00:42:29.060 --> 00:42:31.020
And if you don't know that name, you should
know the name.

00:42:31.020 --> 00:42:38.849
It's pretty bananas how many cultural sort
of milestones this man touched.

00:42:38.849 --> 00:42:43.290
In any case, it seems like a very tough business.

00:42:43.290 --> 00:42:47.619
Even the deal structures have all sorts of
terms that I had never heard before in my

00:42:47.619 --> 00:42:48.619
life.

00:42:48.619 --> 00:42:53.559
It's very different from, say, early stage
tech investing on a multitude of levels, and

00:42:53.559 --> 00:43:05.200
yet it seems like a beautiful, powerful vehicle
and art form in so many respects.

00:43:05.200 --> 00:43:13.079
What would you say were key decisions or events
that informed the success of Fosse?

00:43:13.079 --> 00:43:17.700
David Maisel: I think personal passion in
everything that I've done, when I look back

00:43:17.700 --> 00:43:25.440
at it, I get very focused on whatever project
I'm working on, and I think I do better if

00:43:25.440 --> 00:43:26.970
I really love it.

00:43:26.970 --> 00:43:30.610
And theater and the excitement of live theater,
which you're now experiencing, which is so

00:43:30.610 --> 00:43:40.680
cool with that project, however it works out,
is really indescribably hard to replicate.

00:43:40.680 --> 00:43:45.569
It's a little bit like a live action film
shoot, but more unpredictable.

00:43:45.569 --> 00:43:46.970
There's an audience there.

00:43:46.970 --> 00:43:48.970
It's an animal of its own.

00:43:48.970 --> 00:43:51.010
So that was number one.

00:43:51.010 --> 00:43:57.980
Number two, I enjoy, I guess, telling stories
that have some sort of meaning to me and relevance.

00:43:57.980 --> 00:44:02.460
And this was Bob Fosse, who was a very complicated
man.

00:44:02.460 --> 00:44:07.010
Arguably one of our best choreographers in
America, ever, and probably one of the best

00:44:07.010 --> 00:44:09.160
ever on Broadway.

00:44:09.160 --> 00:44:12.700
And there's a movie called All That Jazz,
which I will recommend to everyone to watch

00:44:12.700 --> 00:44:18.870
if they haven't seen it, with Roy Scheider
and Jessica Lange, which he directed, which

00:44:18.870 --> 00:44:20.440
is really weird.

00:44:20.440 --> 00:44:23.190
Biographical story about himself, because
he predicts his own death.

00:44:23.190 --> 00:44:24.220
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

00:44:24.220 --> 00:44:26.930
David Maisel: He also was a great film director.

00:44:26.930 --> 00:44:31.940
He won the Academy Award for best picture,
I think for Cabaret.

00:44:31.940 --> 00:44:39.440
And when I learned about this guy's life and
saw that he died of whatever substance abuse,

00:44:39.440 --> 00:44:47.230
and womanizing, his lifestyle, and had a very
tragic end, I thought that was a really interesting

00:44:47.230 --> 00:44:48.870
story to tell.

00:44:48.870 --> 00:44:53.920
And then third, he was known for his sensuality
of his dance.

00:44:53.920 --> 00:44:58.180
It was like, whoa, I had never seen anything
like that.

00:44:58.180 --> 00:45:03.190
So the ingredients made me believe that telling
this guy's life story through dance, through

00:45:03.190 --> 00:45:09.260
his own dances could be a really cool Broadway
project.

00:45:09.260 --> 00:45:13.089
I never thought we'd win the Tony for best
musical, because we had no dialogue in the

00:45:13.089 --> 00:45:14.089
show.

00:45:14.089 --> 00:45:19.990
It was literally all his dances and the different
phases of his life.

00:45:19.990 --> 00:45:24.990
The other part I'd say is, and it's sort of
similar, I'm not afraid to use creative end

00:45:24.990 --> 00:45:26.470
business in a decision.

00:45:26.470 --> 00:45:31.869
A lot of people in Broadway or any place in
entertainment are so afraid of that because

00:45:31.869 --> 00:45:35.800
your title is "Suit" if you talk about profits,
and if you want to be creative, you have to

00:45:35.800 --> 00:45:37.790
ignore that and be above all that.

00:45:37.790 --> 00:45:41.510
So the creative mystique is so subjective
that people have.

00:45:41.510 --> 00:45:45.910
In most cases, there's not real accountability
unless you actually draw something or write

00:45:45.910 --> 00:45:47.280
it yourself.

00:45:47.280 --> 00:45:53.180
But I knew that a story, a dance musical,
would be very inexpensive, both its original

00:45:53.180 --> 00:45:57.990
setup costs — the set itself, it's just
basically a black stage and two prosceniums.

00:45:57.990 --> 00:45:59.110
The dancers are — there's no star.

00:45:59.110 --> 00:46:00.560
Tim Ferriss: What was that term you used?

00:46:00.560 --> 00:46:03.250
The blank stage and then —
David Maisel: I think it's proscenium.

00:46:03.250 --> 00:46:05.059
It's like the arches in a theater.

00:46:05.059 --> 00:46:07.140
I hope I'm saying that —
Tim Ferriss: Okay, got it.

00:46:07.140 --> 00:46:08.140
David Maisel: — correctly.

00:46:08.140 --> 00:46:09.140
But we had —
Tim Ferriss: I got the image though.

00:46:09.140 --> 00:46:12.310
David Maisel: Yeah, I think it was like $3
million to set up our set, right?

00:46:12.310 --> 00:46:14.660
Which some musicals are way higher.

00:46:14.660 --> 00:46:19.569
And then the writing cost of the show was
really low because it was 32 amazing dancers,

00:46:19.569 --> 00:46:21.790
but no superstars.

00:46:21.790 --> 00:46:26.040
So it was also a show that was easy to tour,
like you're doing with your show.

00:46:26.040 --> 00:46:31.410
So I understood the economics of Broadway,
and this was a chance — the company that

00:46:31.410 --> 00:46:36.099
Michael had bought was technically, basically
bankrupt, so we didn't have a lot of money

00:46:36.099 --> 00:46:39.250
to spend on a show or market it.

00:46:39.250 --> 00:46:45.490
So out of all the stuff we could do, I saw
that the company owned the rights to this

00:46:45.490 --> 00:46:48.890
project, and I picked that one for us to focus
on.

00:46:48.890 --> 00:46:53.700
So I was producing that at night and running
the company during the day for about two years.

00:46:53.700 --> 00:47:00.180
Tim Ferriss: So for people who don't know,
and wink, wink, might even be me, I'm sure

00:47:00.180 --> 00:47:05.720
there are others out there, the word "producer"
gets used in all sorts of contexts in movies,

00:47:05.720 --> 00:47:09.650
in TV, in theater.

00:47:09.650 --> 00:47:14.290
What does it look like for you at the time
to have been producing at night?

00:47:14.290 --> 00:47:16.020
What were you focused on?

00:47:16.020 --> 00:47:22.339
David Maisel: There's not accountability in
general in entertainment, and titles can mean

00:47:22.339 --> 00:47:24.050
nothing or everything, right?

00:47:24.050 --> 00:47:25.990
So I'm glad you clarified that.

00:47:25.990 --> 00:47:30.240
I don't know what the name is for what I do,
but I've been lucky enough with Fosse, with

00:47:30.240 --> 00:47:37.359
Marvel, with my projects post-Marvel to conceive
of the idea, basically, or see something like

00:47:37.359 --> 00:47:43.930
Marvel that exists that has some legacy, or
Fosse, rights of — and to envision.

00:47:43.930 --> 00:47:47.850
I guess the first step is vision what the
project could be.

00:47:47.850 --> 00:47:50.430
Something that perhaps other people hadn't
seen.

00:47:50.430 --> 00:47:57.650
And then when I produce, it's literally probably
not liked by many people, but it's micromanaging

00:47:57.650 --> 00:48:03.750
all the various relevant major creative decisions
or business decisions, which might include

00:48:03.750 --> 00:48:06.450
bringing in huge creative talent, which I
did on Fosse.

00:48:06.450 --> 00:48:11.000
I brought in his ex-lover, Anne Reinking,
to direct, who had a lot of the dances in

00:48:11.000 --> 00:48:12.130
her head.

00:48:12.130 --> 00:48:15.170
And I was dealing with Gwen Verdon, his ex-wife,
and his daughter, Nicole Fosse.

00:48:15.170 --> 00:48:22.180
But it's very — I like to define it there's
no Fosse musical if I don't make it, and there's

00:48:22.180 --> 00:48:26.670
no MCU or Avengers if I don't go to Marvel.

00:48:26.670 --> 00:48:31.240
So it's having the vision and then going there
and making it happen with whatever dials I

00:48:31.240 --> 00:48:37.240
need to turn, creatively or business or political
or financing, to get it done.

00:48:37.240 --> 00:48:41.890
Tim Ferriss: Just very quickly, we're going
to hone in on this, but MCU, just for people

00:48:41.890 --> 00:48:47.450
who may not have that acronym, just if you
could just say what that is.

00:48:47.450 --> 00:48:52.480
And then looking back at your experience with
Fosse, is there anything that you wish you

00:48:52.480 --> 00:48:58.030
had known then that you know now, anything
you would've done slightly differently, perhaps?

00:48:58.030 --> 00:49:06.230
Those are the —
David Maisel: With Fosse, I was blessed.

00:49:06.230 --> 00:49:10.980
We had our out of town tryouts and then we
went to New York and we won the Tony.

00:49:10.980 --> 00:49:12.730
It doesn't get much better than that for a
show, right?

00:49:12.730 --> 00:49:13.730
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah.

00:49:13.730 --> 00:49:16.099
David Maisel: And I'm trying to think what
would be done differently with that.

00:49:16.099 --> 00:49:20.560
We were able to sell that company, because
of Fosse's success, to Bob Sillerman at the

00:49:20.560 --> 00:49:21.690
time.

00:49:21.690 --> 00:49:26.990
And I remember the investment banker that
worked for me was a guy named Steve Bannon,

00:49:26.990 --> 00:49:32.339
which was odd, but at the time, he had just
come from Goldman Sachs and I only knew him

00:49:32.339 --> 00:49:33.450
as —
Tim Ferriss: What a polymath.

00:49:33.450 --> 00:49:37.710
David Maisel: Yeah, he did a great job as
my banker and got the company sold.

00:49:37.710 --> 00:49:42.430
When his name popped up later on in my life,
I was, "Whoa, is that the same person?"

00:49:42.430 --> 00:49:43.430
and it was.

00:49:43.430 --> 00:49:44.430
So that was sort of funny.

00:49:44.430 --> 00:49:45.430
Tim Ferriss: That's so wild.

00:49:45.430 --> 00:49:46.430
David Maisel: I know.

00:49:46.430 --> 00:49:47.430
Crazy stories.

00:49:47.430 --> 00:49:53.000
But later on, if we want to circle back to
it, I also produced the Spider-Man musical

00:49:53.000 --> 00:49:56.480
on Broadway, which famously lost like 50 or
60 million.

00:49:56.480 --> 00:50:00.859
Tim Ferriss: I was going to say, cost a little
more than three million to put the set together.

00:50:00.859 --> 00:50:05.859
David Maisel: Now, Marvel had no capital in
it, and we had, I think, 10 percent of gross

00:50:05.859 --> 00:50:06.859
ticket sales.

00:50:06.859 --> 00:50:13.240
So we ended up making a significant profit
from that production.

00:50:13.240 --> 00:50:17.680
And it was fantastic to work with Bono and
Edge from U2.

00:50:17.680 --> 00:50:23.430
So there's a whole different animal, the Spider-Man
musical, but I was involved with that as well.

00:50:23.430 --> 00:50:24.430
Yeah.

00:50:24.430 --> 00:50:29.020
Tim Ferriss: Well, let's hit it straight down
the fairway between these two poles.

00:50:29.020 --> 00:50:32.720
And just before we move on, since I promised
folks, MCU, what does that stand for?

00:50:32.720 --> 00:50:34.950
David Maisel: Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:50:34.950 --> 00:50:35.950
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:50:35.950 --> 00:50:43.130
And then we do the Wayne's World, we go back
in time and you know have a few months left

00:50:43.130 --> 00:50:47.260
in working with, if I'm remembering correctly,
Ari and Patrick.

00:50:47.260 --> 00:50:48.540
David Maisel: Yes.

00:50:48.540 --> 00:50:57.850
Tim Ferriss: And somewhere in the next few
months, some of the ideas related to Marvel

00:50:57.850 --> 00:50:59.970
start to percolate.

00:50:59.970 --> 00:51:00.970
What does that look like?

00:51:00.970 --> 00:51:06.119
David Maisel: It actually happened, I remember
specifically, it was in October, November

00:51:06.119 --> 00:51:12.440
of 2003 when I think my stint with Endeavor
was going to finish at the end of that calendar

00:51:12.440 --> 00:51:13.440
year.

00:51:13.440 --> 00:51:18.599
And since that story about the discussion
with Disney to potentially buy Marvel, Marvel

00:51:18.599 --> 00:51:25.730
went bankrupt in 1999, and it had gotten through
a fight between Ron Perelman, Carl Icahn,

00:51:25.730 --> 00:51:27.240
and Ike Perlmutter.

00:51:27.240 --> 00:51:32.250
And Ike Perlmutter successfully got it for
$30 million or $50 million out of bankruptcy.

00:51:32.250 --> 00:51:38.349
And it had been a public company for a couple
years post that, and achieved like a 200 million

00:51:38.349 --> 00:51:42.390
valuation, and I was an investor in the stock.

00:51:42.390 --> 00:51:48.950
So I watched as the first X-Men movie came
out, Spider-Man, of course, right?

00:51:48.950 --> 00:51:56.180
And the stock had sort of stopped at a $200
million valuation.

00:51:56.180 --> 00:52:03.420
I took out some old-fashioned paper and I
thought to myself, "Okay, what will my next

00:52:03.420 --> 00:52:05.190
path be?"

00:52:05.190 --> 00:52:10.520
I could potentially stay at Endeavor and William
Morris for the reasons we talked about.

00:52:10.520 --> 00:52:11.660
It was just Endeavor at the time.

00:52:11.660 --> 00:52:13.210
I decided not to do that.

00:52:13.210 --> 00:52:19.220
Which I think Ari is really happy about because
he very much wanted to buy William Morris

00:52:19.220 --> 00:52:23.319
even back then, and I was telling him all
the reasons not to.

00:52:23.319 --> 00:52:26.790
So I think he was very happy that he didn't
have to listen to those anymore, and he's

00:52:26.790 --> 00:52:29.250
done so well with that acquisition.

00:52:29.250 --> 00:52:30.760
So it worked out really good for both of us.

00:52:30.760 --> 00:52:36.940
I had another path, which was I could go to
one of the studios and try to work my way

00:52:36.940 --> 00:52:42.000
up to be chairman of a studio, which is the
only position I saw where you could be both

00:52:42.000 --> 00:52:43.510
creative and business.

00:52:43.510 --> 00:52:48.530
Those jobs were what I really wanted, chairman
of a studio, because you greenlight movies,

00:52:48.530 --> 00:52:53.440
that's the ultimate power, you decide what
gets made, and then you can influence that

00:52:53.440 --> 00:52:57.170
on every one of those dials that I mentioned,
creative or business.

00:52:57.170 --> 00:53:02.390
And it was the only place where I saw that
that really merged together.

00:53:02.390 --> 00:53:09.290
Some producers had sort of power, but not
as much as a studio chairman.

00:53:09.290 --> 00:53:13.099
So I thought about that path, Tim, and I realized
I didn't like it.

00:53:13.099 --> 00:53:17.910
It wasn't like a law firm or McKinsey, which
is somewhat of a meritocracy.

00:53:17.910 --> 00:53:22.700
Not totally, but at least logically you should
proceed if you deliver results.

00:53:22.700 --> 00:53:25.820
I don't know what the formula is to become
studio chairman.

00:53:25.820 --> 00:53:29.640
It's just one person selected every 10 years.

00:53:29.640 --> 00:53:31.680
They're all in their 60s or 70s.

00:53:31.680 --> 00:53:36.900
And I knew it would be a fool's gamble for
me to do that.

00:53:36.900 --> 00:53:40.040
So then I'm sitting there with this crazy
thought of I'll create my own studio.

00:53:40.040 --> 00:53:44.420
Then, by definition, I'm in charge of that
studio, right?

00:53:44.420 --> 00:53:49.030
And my business, I thought too, it has to
be something I'm really interested in.

00:53:49.030 --> 00:53:54.690
It has to have something that could really
be a sustainable, good business, because I

00:53:54.690 --> 00:53:56.940
needed —
Tim Ferriss: Can I ask you a dumb question

00:53:56.940 --> 00:53:57.940
just as an interjection?

00:53:57.940 --> 00:53:58.940
David Maisel: Yeah.

00:53:58.940 --> 00:54:01.750
Tim Ferriss: Because I feel like it's my job
to do that.

00:54:01.750 --> 00:54:07.410
I just realized, as you're talking, I have
heard and used this term "studio" myself many

00:54:07.410 --> 00:54:11.390
times, and I don't actually know exactly what
it means to be a studio.

00:54:11.390 --> 00:54:12.390
David Maisel: Yeah, good question.

00:54:12.390 --> 00:54:18.700
Tim Ferriss: Do they raise money and fund
production and then external production companies

00:54:18.700 --> 00:54:21.869
and teams kind of take that money and run
with it?

00:54:21.869 --> 00:54:24.530
How much is internal versus external?

00:54:24.530 --> 00:54:27.120
If you started your own studio, what would
that entail?

00:54:27.120 --> 00:54:32.600
David Maisel: So what a studio should mean
is that you bring all the elements to the

00:54:32.600 --> 00:54:33.600
table.

00:54:33.600 --> 00:54:37.430
You have the money, you have the properties,
you have people that either make the films

00:54:37.430 --> 00:54:38.480
or hire producers.

00:54:38.480 --> 00:54:40.740
Tim Ferriss: Properties meaning the intellectual
property.

00:54:40.740 --> 00:54:41.980
David Maisel: Exactly.

00:54:41.980 --> 00:54:46.340
You either make the movies yourself or you
internal producers or you hire other producers

00:54:46.340 --> 00:54:47.340
to do it.

00:54:47.340 --> 00:54:51.680
And then you market and distribute the films,
which costs as much as making the movies for

00:54:51.680 --> 00:54:54.299
a major picture.

00:54:54.299 --> 00:55:03.440
So at the time, there was six studios, Universal,
Disney, Fox, and so on, Sony, and they made

00:55:03.440 --> 00:55:04.869
all the movies.

00:55:04.869 --> 00:55:11.740
So every IP holder then and even now would
license their properties to these studios.

00:55:11.740 --> 00:55:15.599
So Spider-Man was famously licensed to Sony.

00:55:15.599 --> 00:55:20.309
Sony would make the decision when there's
a movie, how much they're going to spend,

00:55:20.309 --> 00:55:25.970
creatively what it was going to be, and market
and distribute that movie and keep the vast,

00:55:25.970 --> 00:55:30.220
not the majority, but like 95 percent plus
of profits.

00:55:30.220 --> 00:55:35.220
So an IP holder like Marvel would only get
a piece of the money and they'd have script

00:55:35.220 --> 00:55:39.830
notes, like certain rights, but nothing very
heavy.

00:55:39.830 --> 00:55:46.010
Like you couldn't make Peter Parker do some
crazy job that wasn't relevant to the comics

00:55:46.010 --> 00:55:49.150
or something like that.

00:55:49.150 --> 00:55:53.680
And then normally, ancillaries, like toys,
would be split between the studio and the

00:55:53.680 --> 00:55:54.680
rights holder.

00:55:54.680 --> 00:55:59.980
So the way Marvel got to a $200 million valuation
was off the toys of Spider-Man, or the toys

00:55:59.980 --> 00:56:02.920
of Hulk, or the toys of X-Men.

00:56:02.920 --> 00:56:05.670
Very little money from the movies.

00:56:05.670 --> 00:56:07.990
I was frustrated with that as an investor.

00:56:07.990 --> 00:56:12.760
I didn't think it was a good model to grow
enterprise value.

00:56:12.760 --> 00:56:15.680
So I was very focused on Marvel right away.

00:56:15.680 --> 00:56:17.569
So luckily, I didn't have to look at my second
choice.

00:56:17.569 --> 00:56:19.750
I knew I was personally interested in it.

00:56:19.750 --> 00:56:25.070
And then I had this hypothesis, or model,
at the time, which was I knew how bad the

00:56:25.070 --> 00:56:26.849
first movie business was.

00:56:26.849 --> 00:56:31.289
You spend, in this case, a hundred million
dollars on something, you hope people show

00:56:31.289 --> 00:56:32.289
up.

00:56:32.289 --> 00:56:35.140
Yeah, research can help you a little bit,
but very little.

00:56:35.140 --> 00:56:36.270
It's pretty random.

00:56:36.270 --> 00:56:39.890
Stars at the time could maybe open the movie.

00:56:39.890 --> 00:56:43.460
But it's a horrible, not a horrible, but a
pretty bad business.

00:56:43.460 --> 00:56:48.799
But the sequel business was amazing because
statistically, you could predict the revenues

00:56:48.799 --> 00:56:54.360
plus or minus something, and you could manage
your profit margin, not whether you made profits.

00:56:54.360 --> 00:56:59.270
So the big idea I had, which was now is called
the Universe, was what if after the first

00:56:59.270 --> 00:57:05.231
movie, every movie after that was a sequel
or a quasi-sequel, which required all the

00:57:05.231 --> 00:57:10.570
characters, or a lot of the characters, to
show up in multiple movies?

00:57:10.570 --> 00:57:15.700
That led me, again, to Marvel because I knew
about the vast universe of characters, and

00:57:15.700 --> 00:57:23.349
that this would be a perfect way, in my mind,
to make every movie after the first one a

00:57:23.349 --> 00:57:25.619
sequel, or a quasi-sequel.

00:57:25.619 --> 00:57:29.849
So it came up over 24, 48 hours that weekend,
the whole thing.

00:57:29.849 --> 00:57:32.400
Tim Ferriss: Were you sitting in an ashram?

00:57:32.400 --> 00:57:36.200
Were you flying on airplanes?

00:57:36.200 --> 00:57:39.660
Were you having —
David Maisel: No, no, no.

00:57:39.660 --> 00:57:40.770
I was —
Tim Ferriss: — endoscopy?

00:57:40.770 --> 00:57:41.770
What was going on?

00:57:41.770 --> 00:57:45.930
David Maisel: It was very boring, sweatpants
sitting in my rental apartment across from

00:57:45.930 --> 00:57:49.819
the park near Fairfax where I still have my
studio.

00:57:49.819 --> 00:57:50.819
Same building.

00:57:50.819 --> 00:57:51.819
Yeah.

00:57:51.819 --> 00:57:52.819
Yeah.

00:57:52.819 --> 00:57:55.289
Tim Ferriss: Okay, so you're capturing all
this on a yellow notepad or —

00:57:55.289 --> 00:57:56.289
David Maisel: Yes.

00:57:56.289 --> 00:57:58.450
Tim Ferriss: — in your laptop or wherever.

00:57:58.450 --> 00:57:59.730
Then what?

00:57:59.730 --> 00:58:00.770
So it's great idea.

00:58:00.770 --> 00:58:02.310
I could see how this works.

00:58:02.310 --> 00:58:04.680
David Maisel: That was the good news.

00:58:04.680 --> 00:58:08.460
The bad news was I didn't know anyone at Marvel.

00:58:08.460 --> 00:58:12.510
I knew Marvel had licensed a lot of its properties
like Spider-Man, X-Men.

00:58:12.510 --> 00:58:16.200
I didn't know how many that they had licensed
beyond that.

00:58:16.200 --> 00:58:21.250
I had never made a movie before in my life.

00:58:21.250 --> 00:58:24.160
So there's a lot of things that were obstacles.

00:58:24.160 --> 00:58:33.960
And in the next month or two, I got an introduction
to a man named Avi Arad and Ike Perlmutter

00:58:33.960 --> 00:58:37.329
who were the two guys running Marvel, the
public company at the time.

00:58:37.329 --> 00:58:39.510
Tim Ferriss: How did you get that introduction?

00:58:39.510 --> 00:58:40.510
This is not trivial.

00:58:40.510 --> 00:58:41.510
David Maisel: Yeah.

00:58:41.510 --> 00:58:43.230
It's shows you how small this town is.

00:58:43.230 --> 00:58:49.500
My lawyer who negotiated my deal with Ari
Emanuel in Endeavor was a man named Josh Grode,

00:58:49.500 --> 00:58:52.470
who was also Avi Arad's lawyer at the time.

00:58:52.470 --> 00:58:57.119
And I told my lawyer my interest and he said,
"Oh, I can introduce you to Avi."

00:58:57.119 --> 00:59:05.310
Now, to show you how this town is really small,
Josh is now the CEO and president of Legendary

00:59:05.310 --> 00:59:10.710
Entertainment, which is a big mini studio
owned by, a lot, by the Chinese.

00:59:10.710 --> 00:59:15.760
And so he's become his own studio chairman
and pretty powerful company with the King

00:59:15.760 --> 00:59:17.580
Kong movies and other things like that.

00:59:17.580 --> 00:59:18.600
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:59:18.600 --> 00:59:20.800
David Maisel: So you mentioned studio.

00:59:20.800 --> 00:59:25.950
At the time, Avi was in charge of what was
called Marvel Studios, but it was really a

00:59:25.950 --> 00:59:30.790
small shop of giving script notes to license
movies.

00:59:30.790 --> 00:59:38.280
Avi successfully saw the potential of Marvel
films and started licensing the movies post

00:59:38.280 --> 00:59:40.390
the bankruptcy.

00:59:40.390 --> 00:59:46.840
Ike and Avi had known each other for many
years, and Avi started as a toy creator and

00:59:46.840 --> 00:59:49.250
he did the original license deal with Spider-Man.

00:59:49.250 --> 00:59:55.270
He did the original license deal with Fox
for X-Men, and that's what the route that

00:59:55.270 --> 01:00:02.090
Marvel was on at the time was licensing these
movies and continuing to license their films.

01:00:02.090 --> 01:00:07.349
Ike owned, I think, 40 or 50 percent of Marvel
at the time, even though it was a public company,

01:00:07.349 --> 01:00:10.660
and he was in charge of everything.

01:00:10.660 --> 01:00:12.069
Tim Ferriss: How uncommon is that?

01:00:12.069 --> 01:00:14.000
That seems like a gigantic equity position.

01:00:14.000 --> 01:00:18.550
David Maisel: It was, yeah, because he got
it out of company bankruptcy.

01:00:18.550 --> 01:00:21.349
He got out of bankruptcy and he kept it.

01:00:21.349 --> 01:00:26.210
And so Ike was the person I really needed
to pitch, and I pitched him at Mar-a-Lago

01:00:26.210 --> 01:00:35.430
in November of 2003, and I remember that flying
to Palm Beach and having lunch there, and

01:00:35.430 --> 01:00:38.650
Trump came by, was close with Ike.

01:00:38.650 --> 01:00:39.760
They're still very close.

01:00:39.760 --> 01:00:46.410
And I was introduced to Trump, and Trump said
something about he had a TV show starting

01:00:46.410 --> 01:00:49.770
soon, which turned out to be The Apprentice,
and he was excited about that.

01:00:49.770 --> 01:00:52.819
That's all I remember from that conversation.

01:00:52.819 --> 01:00:59.260
And I pitched Ike in the same way I pitched
Ovitz and Ike did not see and agree that they

01:00:59.260 --> 01:01:05.160
should do their own films right away, but
he was open-minded enough to offer me a job

01:01:05.160 --> 01:01:15.510
and give me a lot of stock options at market
and give me a chance to fight for it internally.

01:01:15.510 --> 01:01:21.020
And Ike, he is now, I think, the largest individual
shareholder of Disney.

01:01:21.020 --> 01:01:23.320
Tim Ferriss: That's incredible.

01:01:23.320 --> 01:01:25.040
David Maisel: Yeah.

01:01:25.040 --> 01:01:27.330
Tim Ferriss: All right.

01:01:27.330 --> 01:01:29.049
You can fight this fight.

01:01:29.049 --> 01:01:34.559
I don't necessarily agree with all of it up
front, but yeah, you'll have your day in court,

01:01:34.559 --> 01:01:35.559
so to speak.

01:01:35.559 --> 01:01:36.650
David Maisel: Exactly.

01:01:36.650 --> 01:01:37.650
That was it.

01:01:37.650 --> 01:01:40.869
Tim Ferriss: And what then proceeds to happen
from there?

01:01:40.869 --> 01:01:45.609
David Maisel: I had one other job that I was
looking at, which I'm only going to raise,

01:01:45.609 --> 01:01:49.760
and I've never told this publicly because
of your question about theater, but I got

01:01:49.760 --> 01:01:55.609
a phone call in the fall of 2003 from a very
high-pitched voice who said, "Hi, this is

01:01:55.609 --> 01:01:57.890
Michael Jackson."

01:01:57.890 --> 01:02:03.030
And he went on to say that he had seen Fosse
on Broadway and loved the show, and it was

01:02:03.030 --> 01:02:04.030
a personal call.

01:02:04.030 --> 01:02:08.859
I don't know how he got my phone number and
I thought it was fake.

01:02:08.859 --> 01:02:13.900
And he said, "I want you to tell my life story
through my music videos."

01:02:13.900 --> 01:02:15.480
And I was like, "Whoa."

01:02:15.480 --> 01:02:22.720
And that turned into a one or two month set
of discussions with Michael about doing a

01:02:22.720 --> 01:02:28.480
50/50 deal where I'd have — he put in all
his music videos and all the IP.

01:02:28.480 --> 01:02:30.799
I'd be able to produce it.

01:02:30.799 --> 01:02:34.090
And we both had vetoes on each other.

01:02:34.090 --> 01:02:39.050
And I got him to agree that he would perform,
randomly once every couple months, Thriller.

01:02:39.050 --> 01:02:43.369
So you'd go to the Broadway show and you'd
see Michael Jackson actually perform — be

01:02:43.369 --> 01:02:46.170
like a lottery.

01:02:46.170 --> 01:02:47.640
And Josh Grode was my lawyer on that.

01:02:47.640 --> 01:02:48.640
So I have the deal agreement.

01:02:48.640 --> 01:02:51.349
Tim Ferriss: I was going to say that'd be
tricky to figure out from the legal perspective.

01:02:51.349 --> 01:02:53.160
David Maisel: But he was very smart.

01:02:53.160 --> 01:02:57.130
I've got to tell you, he was very smart business
person.

01:02:57.130 --> 01:03:03.010
And then I wasn't really, seriously, I was
going to carve that out of my Marvel deal,

01:03:03.010 --> 01:03:06.170
and I think Marvel was going to let me do
that.

01:03:06.170 --> 01:03:12.210
And then I saw Michael get arrested, and so
I let that fizzle right at that period of

01:03:12.210 --> 01:03:15.520
time, but it was like the other thing that
I was looking at.

01:03:15.520 --> 01:03:21.050
And then third, Hef was circling me about
potentially helping him run Playboy at the

01:03:21.050 --> 01:03:27.640
time, which was an IP company, but Marvel
was the part where my focus was the highest.

01:03:27.640 --> 01:03:34.270
And I started right away basically two weeks
after that meeting with Ike at the end of

01:03:34.270 --> 01:03:35.270
2003.

01:03:35.270 --> 01:03:36.750
Tim Ferriss: In title, what was your job?

01:03:36.750 --> 01:03:42.569
David Maisel: My title was President of what
was then called Marvel Studios, which wasn't

01:03:42.569 --> 01:03:43.579
a studio.

01:03:43.579 --> 01:03:44.859
We didn't bring any money.

01:03:44.859 --> 01:03:46.890
We didn't have distribution in marketing.

01:03:46.890 --> 01:03:52.200
We weren't making the movies, but it was called
Marvel Studios from a title point of view.

01:03:52.200 --> 01:03:53.200
Yeah.

01:03:53.200 --> 01:03:54.200
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:03:54.200 --> 01:04:02.420
So he put you in the position where you could,
at least without having to lily pad hop around

01:04:02.420 --> 01:04:05.339
the company, implement what you'd been discussing?

01:04:05.339 --> 01:04:06.339
David Maisel: Yeah, it was 2004.

01:04:06.339 --> 01:04:10.960
So this part we're going to get into, which
I'm happy to do.

01:04:10.960 --> 01:04:16.440
Like you said in the beginning, I've never
talked through in this way and I've also not

01:04:16.440 --> 01:04:19.660
done press of any significant note in the
past 10 years.

01:04:19.660 --> 01:04:25.180
So a large amount of this is still coming
to light.

01:04:25.180 --> 01:04:29.319
And so some things people might know and some
things they might not.

01:04:29.319 --> 01:04:33.809
But in 2004, we had a very small office, on
Santa Monica Boulevard.

01:04:33.809 --> 01:04:39.250
I think Ike, he was definitely interested
in making more money from entertainment.

01:04:39.250 --> 01:04:46.900
And so my job was really how can we make more
money without taking any significant risk?

01:04:46.900 --> 01:04:53.619
Had a career of bankruptcy type of companies,
Remington and now Marvel, and those people

01:04:53.619 --> 01:04:57.720
are not ones that take big capital risk on
big grandiose visions.

01:04:57.720 --> 01:05:02.880
And the board of directors was a bunch of
ex-bankruptcy guys, basically.

01:05:02.880 --> 01:05:06.369
And Sid Ganis, I remember, who was the president
of the Academy of Motion Pictures, was the

01:05:06.369 --> 01:05:09.200
one Hollywood person there.

01:05:09.200 --> 01:05:21.839
And so I would argue for the studio, in the
board meetings and was basically told to be

01:05:21.839 --> 01:05:23.760
quiet, maybe not those words.

01:05:23.760 --> 01:05:30.099
My last name wasn't Spielberg and not to bring
it up again unless Marvel had no risk.

01:05:30.099 --> 01:05:33.160
Not little risk, but no risk.

01:05:33.160 --> 01:05:36.799
And so —
Tim Ferriss: Conjure me this, Spielberg.

01:05:36.799 --> 01:05:41.520
David Maisel: And what was important at the
time is I asked the board to at least give

01:05:41.520 --> 01:05:42.690
me six months.

01:05:42.690 --> 01:05:46.420
And what that meant is not to license anymore
movies.

01:05:46.420 --> 01:05:50.920
They were about to license Captain America
and Thor to two major studios, I think it

01:05:50.920 --> 01:05:55.359
was Warner Bros. and Sony.

01:05:55.359 --> 01:05:59.370
And Thor specifically was very close to being
licensed.

01:05:59.370 --> 01:06:04.350
And I basically had to hold my breath in the
board meeting and say, "If you do that, I

01:06:04.350 --> 01:06:05.350
can't do anything.

01:06:05.350 --> 01:06:12.470
I need at least those characters to put together
something that somebody would finance as a

01:06:12.470 --> 01:06:14.609
slate."

01:06:14.609 --> 01:06:16.670
And they gave me that six-month time.

01:06:16.670 --> 01:06:22.740
And so during 2004, I put on my MBA hat, I
spent a lot of time in New York, and was able

01:06:22.740 --> 01:06:28.750
to raise the financing that we needed — was
about a half a billion dollars of what turned

01:06:28.750 --> 01:06:33.270
out to be, I mean, it sounds crazy to say,
but for those that are interested, it was

01:06:33.270 --> 01:06:34.270
no risk.

01:06:34.270 --> 01:06:35.270
It was non-recourse.

01:06:35.270 --> 01:06:37.460
Marvel never had to pay it back in no collateral.

01:06:37.460 --> 01:06:40.000
Tim Ferriss: So, two things, how do you sell?

01:06:40.000 --> 01:06:44.530
How do you pitch that and then how do you
structure that?

01:06:44.530 --> 01:06:51.980
David Maisel: The structuring, it's related,
it was this idea of the universe, again, not

01:06:51.980 --> 01:06:57.260
a word that we used at the time, but it was
the vision of intermixing all the characters.

01:06:57.260 --> 01:07:03.960
First to my board, the argument, was if we
own our own studio, it means we get the full

01:07:03.960 --> 01:07:07.910
financial upside that they understood very
well.

01:07:07.910 --> 01:07:12.329
Number two, we decide on greenlight when the
movies get made that they also understood

01:07:12.329 --> 01:07:17.569
because they only sold toys really at the
time, and the toys were contingent on a movie,

01:07:17.569 --> 01:07:19.359
which they then control the timing.

01:07:19.359 --> 01:07:22.670
Now when you're doing a public company and
you're giving guidance every year, how can

01:07:22.670 --> 01:07:25.760
you give guidance if you don't even know what
movies are going to get made?

01:07:25.760 --> 01:07:30.369
And so controlling greenlight was important,
full creative control.

01:07:30.369 --> 01:07:37.720
I felt that people like myself and Kevin Feige
eventually, because we care about the movies,

01:07:37.720 --> 01:07:40.049
would make better movies for the fans.

01:07:40.049 --> 01:07:45.950
I'm not sure my board really heard that point
or really understood it, given that neither

01:07:45.950 --> 01:07:50.240
Kevin or I had ever made a movie ourselves,
like I said.

01:07:50.240 --> 01:07:54.420
And then fourth, we would keep all the ancillaries
as well, the toys and video games.

01:07:54.420 --> 01:07:59.599
So there was a lot of reasons for this to
happen.

01:07:59.599 --> 01:08:03.809
The challenge of not having risk is normally
insurmountable.

01:08:03.809 --> 01:08:05.410
Thank God again for timing.

01:08:05.410 --> 01:08:11.559
Like we talked about, the bond bubble of 2004
was happening, so it was a time where there

01:08:11.559 --> 01:08:13.880
was loans being made that shouldn't have been
made.

01:08:13.880 --> 01:08:19.250
And a lot of people were enamored with Hollywood
as they get enamored every few years.

01:08:19.250 --> 01:08:26.270
And so I was able to pitch that, give me four
at bats, and if one of them hits, then every

01:08:26.270 --> 01:08:27.850
movie's a sequel after that.

01:08:27.850 --> 01:08:32.040
And when they ran their models, they were
able to get it past their investment committee

01:08:32.040 --> 01:08:34.330
to the point where there was no equity component.

01:08:34.330 --> 01:08:35.520
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

01:08:35.520 --> 01:08:36.520
All right.

01:08:36.520 --> 01:08:40.540
Just so I understand, there are many ways
to raise financing.

01:08:40.540 --> 01:08:42.420
So are we talking about banks?

01:08:42.420 --> 01:08:45.980
Are we talking about if so, what types of
banks are we talking about?

01:08:45.980 --> 01:08:46.980
David Maisel: Yeah.

01:08:46.980 --> 01:08:51.699
Tim Ferriss: Who are the people you were pitching
to agree to these terms and provide the financing?

01:08:51.699 --> 01:08:57.109
David Maisel: Pitched a lot of different entities,
but turned out to be Merrill Lynch who took

01:08:57.109 --> 01:09:02.089
the gamble with us and helped structure it,
then they offloaded, and it was structured,

01:09:02.089 --> 01:09:03.750
as I mentioned, as pure debt.

01:09:03.750 --> 01:09:06.170
So very low interest rate.

01:09:06.170 --> 01:09:12.060
And the only collateral were the film rights
to 10 Marvel characters of which we could

01:09:12.060 --> 01:09:13.920
make for the movies.

01:09:13.920 --> 01:09:21.540
And a lot of people misunderstand that they
think we pledged 10 of our characters as collateral.

01:09:21.540 --> 01:09:27.000
It wasn't that at all because in the worst
case scenario, it only got collected if we

01:09:27.000 --> 01:09:29.170
lost money on those first four movies.

01:09:29.170 --> 01:09:34.540
And then those six characters, we owned all
the rights besides film.

01:09:34.540 --> 01:09:41.640
And if a film was ever made by the bank, whoever
collected this collateral, we got the same

01:09:41.640 --> 01:09:46.520
license fee that we get if we just license
it that day to a party.

01:09:46.520 --> 01:09:48.130
So there was no opportunity cost.

01:09:48.130 --> 01:09:51.020
Tim Ferriss: So that's how you would justify
it to your board of directors?

01:09:51.020 --> 01:09:52.020
David Maisel: Exactly.

01:09:52.020 --> 01:09:53.020
Tim Ferriss: Also?

01:09:53.020 --> 01:09:54.020
David Maisel: Right, exactly.

01:09:54.020 --> 01:09:57.040
And not only that —
Tim Ferriss: Worst case, we get what we would've

01:09:57.040 --> 01:09:59.120
gotten doing our current model?

01:09:59.120 --> 01:10:03.500
David Maisel: And actually even better because
we were able to get five percent of a hundred

01:10:03.500 --> 01:10:05.840
percent revenues as a producer fee.

01:10:05.840 --> 01:10:11.929
So even if the film lost money, we were making
10 to 20 million, and the bank let us keep

01:10:11.929 --> 01:10:16.660
all of the non film revenues, like toys and
video games in a different waterfall.

01:10:16.660 --> 01:10:21.480
So even if the four at bats lost, we were
making 25 million per movie, and that's a

01:10:21.480 --> 01:10:23.670
hundred million dollars, and the whole company
was worth 200.

01:10:23.670 --> 01:10:29.670
So it was not a deal that the board could
say no to, but they also got enthusiastic

01:10:29.670 --> 01:10:31.860
about it by the end of 2004.

01:10:31.860 --> 01:10:33.270
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:10:33.270 --> 01:10:40.450
So were there any hurdles with the board,
or was it just so clearly delivering on the

01:10:40.450 --> 01:10:47.480
request of zero risk that there was just no
defensible position they could take that would

01:10:47.480 --> 01:10:48.590
object to this?

01:10:48.590 --> 01:10:51.719
David Maisel: Yeah, eventually when the financing
came together.

01:10:51.719 --> 01:10:56.910
But it's important for Marvel fans to realize
that it was tender at the time.

01:10:56.910 --> 01:11:04.420
If we were not able to raise riskless capital
like that, the MCU might never have occurred.

01:11:04.420 --> 01:11:09.080
Or if I got there six months later and those
other movies were licensed, it would've been

01:11:09.080 --> 01:11:11.120
too late, anyways.

01:11:11.120 --> 01:11:16.989
The idea, by the way, of Marvel or any IP
holder making their own hundred million dollar-plus

01:11:16.989 --> 01:11:18.610
movie was revolutionary.

01:11:18.610 --> 01:11:25.370
Like nobody to this day, I'm only aware of
Marvel and Angry Birds who's done that, and

01:11:25.370 --> 01:11:27.420
Angry Birds I was part of after I left.

01:11:27.420 --> 01:11:29.520
So it was a crazy idea, Tim.

01:11:29.520 --> 01:11:33.130
And to Ike's credit, he knew he did give me
that audience.

01:11:33.130 --> 01:11:41.400
He sent me an email that I have behind me
recently thanking me for my tenacity and basically

01:11:41.400 --> 01:11:44.940
said — can I actually read it?

01:11:44.940 --> 01:11:46.130
One second.

01:11:46.130 --> 01:11:49.739
"This was after Avengers: Endgame in 2019.

01:11:49.739 --> 01:11:56.020
David, the movie was a terrific accomplishment,
and we know that it all started with you,

01:11:56.020 --> 01:12:00.020
your vision, and your tenacity and pushing
to achieve that vision."

01:12:00.020 --> 01:12:03.360
The word tenacity is about those hurdles you
just mentioned, right?

01:12:03.360 --> 01:12:04.880
It was tough.

01:12:04.880 --> 01:12:12.670
I mean, that was a huge hurdle to get over
having the correct financing that would work.

01:12:12.670 --> 01:12:16.100
I have never said this publicly.

01:12:16.100 --> 01:12:21.090
I could have showed up in the boardroom and
given up half the equity and the same terms

01:12:21.090 --> 01:12:26.250
for the other 50 percent of the money, and
that would've been easier to structure, and

01:12:26.250 --> 01:12:28.650
perhaps my board would not have cared.

01:12:28.650 --> 01:12:33.330
Half is better than nothing of the movie,
and I was very greedy.

01:12:33.330 --> 01:12:37.141
I wanted Marvel to keep a hundred percent
of the upside because also I was one of the

01:12:37.141 --> 01:12:40.960
largest individual owners at that time with
my stock options.

01:12:40.960 --> 01:12:47.540
And so I was very tenacious with Ike, but
I was also very tenacious with Merrill Lynch

01:12:47.540 --> 01:12:53.120
that we keep a hundred percent of the upside
and no deal is easy.

01:12:53.120 --> 01:12:59.420
They tried to renegotiate and make us put
up a third of the budget some other way.

01:12:59.420 --> 01:13:05.870
I was fortunate, I guess, to be able to convince
them not to do that at the end.

01:13:05.870 --> 01:13:06.980
So it was very prepared.

01:13:06.980 --> 01:13:12.110
Tim Ferriss: And this was renegotiating years
later after they had signed the dotted line,

01:13:12.110 --> 01:13:13.110
or this was mid-process?

01:13:13.110 --> 01:13:14.110
David Maisel: Yeah, we closed.

01:13:14.110 --> 01:13:17.790
We probably came to agreement in the fall
of '04 and closed it in the spring of '05.

01:13:17.790 --> 01:13:20.810
And things always get renegotiated until they're
closed.

01:13:20.810 --> 01:13:22.989
It's the best way to put it.

01:13:22.989 --> 01:13:23.989
Yeah.

01:13:23.989 --> 01:13:28.240
Now all those guys, it turned out to be one
of their best loans they ever made, obviously.

01:13:28.240 --> 01:13:30.449
And I'm friends with a lot of those bankers
still.

01:13:30.449 --> 01:13:35.980
It was an interesting time, but that was only
one of the things, because even with the money

01:13:35.980 --> 01:13:41.380
that came in, we still needed a distribution
deal with a studio.

01:13:41.380 --> 01:13:48.340
All that money was contingent on a major studio
putting up 150 million bucks to market the

01:13:48.340 --> 01:13:49.340
right.

01:13:49.340 --> 01:13:52.060
Tim Ferriss: Before we get there, just a very
quick question.

01:13:52.060 --> 01:13:55.250
Keeping in mind I'm slow-moving from Long
Island.

01:13:55.250 --> 01:13:56.250
David Maisel: No worries.

01:13:56.250 --> 01:13:57.670
Tim Ferriss: Takes me a minute or two.

01:13:57.670 --> 01:13:59.989
So I'm thinking of the numbers here.

01:13:59.989 --> 01:14:05.420
Company is worth, as a publicly traded company,
yeah, I think you said 200 million, right?

01:14:05.420 --> 01:14:06.980
200 million market cap.

01:14:06.980 --> 01:14:09.880
You're raising 500 million.

01:14:09.880 --> 01:14:16.960
To do that, I would love to know what the
most important exhibits were and how the licensed

01:14:16.960 --> 01:14:22.239
films like X-Men and Spider-Man fit into that,
if at all, right?

01:14:22.239 --> 01:14:26.020
Because otherwise it strikes me that it would
be perhaps based on industry comps, but it

01:14:26.020 --> 01:14:29.790
would be considered potentially very speculative.

01:14:29.790 --> 01:14:35.710
David Maisel: I think a lot of people are
now looking and learning that Marvel Studios

01:14:35.710 --> 01:14:37.460
was a relay race.

01:14:37.460 --> 01:14:43.699
Stan Lee and the original comic creators created
the characters, and then the baton went to

01:14:43.699 --> 01:14:48.770
Ike and Avi and Avi specifically doing these
license deals, and Ike supported him.

01:14:48.770 --> 01:14:53.530
And then the baton in 2003 went to me, and
then we can get to later on, I passed the

01:14:53.530 --> 01:14:59.800
baton to Kevin Feige in 2010, and he's still
running the studio.

01:14:59.800 --> 01:15:07.520
If I didn't have the numbers of X-Men and
Spider-Man to point to and run scenarios to

01:15:07.520 --> 01:15:14.090
my board internally and to the bank that what
if we just do half of those numbers or two-thirds

01:15:14.090 --> 01:15:16.170
of those numbers for their stress case scenarios?

01:15:16.170 --> 01:15:21.190
Yeah, I mean, they never ran a stress case
scenario at doing a third of those numbers.

01:15:21.190 --> 01:15:23.270
If they did, we would've failed.

01:15:23.270 --> 01:15:25.380
So they wanted this deal to happen.

01:15:25.380 --> 01:15:27.860
And there's incentives.

01:15:27.860 --> 01:15:30.380
We go back to the street smarts of Mike Ovitz,
right?

01:15:30.380 --> 01:15:31.750
What does Merrill want out of this?

01:15:31.750 --> 01:15:33.590
What does the bank want?

01:15:33.590 --> 01:15:39.750
Well, they got, I think, a three percent success
fee with this deal, and they held the debt

01:15:39.750 --> 01:15:43.520
for all of probably a half hour until they
flipped it to somebody else.

01:15:43.520 --> 01:15:51.770
So there was incentives for everyone to get
this deal done.

01:15:51.770 --> 01:15:53.810
And that was important to figure out.

01:15:53.810 --> 01:15:56.200
Tim Ferriss: Dumb question, who's paying the
three percent?

01:15:56.200 --> 01:15:58.320
David Maisel: It comes out of the facility.

01:15:58.320 --> 01:15:59.370
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:15:59.370 --> 01:16:00.370
Yeah.

01:16:00.370 --> 01:16:01.409
Need you to expand on that.

01:16:01.409 --> 01:16:07.340
David Maisel: So I've raised 525 million and
three percent of that, about 17 million —

01:16:07.340 --> 01:16:08.340
Tim Ferriss: I see.

01:16:08.340 --> 01:16:10.270
David Maisel: — went to Merrill Lynch right
away.

01:16:10.270 --> 01:16:12.860
Tim Ferriss: And who are they raising it from?

01:16:12.860 --> 01:16:17.040
I'm just wondering if they're taking it out
of one pocket and sticking it in the other

01:16:17.040 --> 01:16:18.040
—
David Maisel: Yeah.

01:16:18.040 --> 01:16:19.040
Tim Ferriss: — what that means.

01:16:19.040 --> 01:16:22.040
David Maisel: A lot of times, and I learned
this in real time doing this, we really didn't

01:16:22.040 --> 01:16:27.380
have a banker helping us on this deal too
much.

01:16:27.380 --> 01:16:32.960
And so it was a man named John Turitzin, who
was the general counsel of Marvel, and who

01:16:32.960 --> 01:16:37.750
is important to mention because he was the
closest person and still is to Ike.

01:16:37.750 --> 01:16:43.610
And he kept my head in the game, and he helped
me get through this process.

01:16:43.610 --> 01:16:48.370
And many times, I thought of quitting and
Ike probably thought about firing me.

01:16:48.370 --> 01:16:51.690
I know he talked us both off the ledge many
times.

01:16:51.690 --> 01:16:54.960
So if it wasn't for John, there's no Marvel
Studios either.

01:16:54.960 --> 01:16:58.860
I'm either fired or I quit and probably fired.

01:16:58.860 --> 01:17:03.050
But I guess it's a pretty detailed thing,
but I was very greedy.

01:17:03.050 --> 01:17:07.590
I wanted the interest rate to be lower, and
we got it insured by a company called the

01:17:07.590 --> 01:17:09.520
MPAC, most of the debt.

01:17:09.520 --> 01:17:15.340
So it became AAA debt, which is then easy
to sell to pensions and easy to sell to individual

01:17:15.340 --> 01:17:16.340
investors.

01:17:16.340 --> 01:17:17.340
Tim Ferriss: Right.

01:17:17.340 --> 01:17:18.340
David Maisel: Yeah.

01:17:18.340 --> 01:17:19.340
Tim Ferriss: Right.

01:17:19.340 --> 01:17:20.840
So Merrill turned around and it was packaged
with AAA, and they were able to resell it.

01:17:20.840 --> 01:17:21.940
David Maisel: Yes.

01:17:21.940 --> 01:17:24.330
Tim Ferriss: I'm sorry to belabor this point.

01:17:24.330 --> 01:17:30.330
Again, my podcast is basically my continuing
education for Tim Ferriss program.

01:17:30.330 --> 01:17:37.730
So coming out of the facility is Merrill,
are they basically reselling it and then taking

01:17:37.730 --> 01:17:40.150
their three percent after they have resold
it?

01:17:40.150 --> 01:17:44.960
I guess, I'm just trying to figure out where
the success fee, how it comes out of the facility.

01:17:44.960 --> 01:17:51.380
David Maisel: I think they took risk because
at the time the deal closes, they're obligated

01:17:51.380 --> 01:17:53.590
to put up the 525.

01:17:53.590 --> 01:17:56.389
So they got their success fee, I think, right
at that time.

01:17:56.389 --> 01:18:00.810
And then they resell most of it to other people
pretty immediately.

01:18:00.810 --> 01:18:01.810
So I don't know.

01:18:01.810 --> 01:18:07.000
I'm trying to remember back, this was 2003,
2004, 2005, but they had risk when the deal

01:18:07.000 --> 01:18:11.280
closed for, I don't know if it was for half
hour or for one day or for two days, but they

01:18:11.280 --> 01:18:14.280
had risk for a period of time before they
resold all that.

01:18:14.280 --> 01:18:17.780
And that's when they get their success fee
once the money's guaranteed us.

01:18:17.780 --> 01:18:24.449
Tim Ferriss: Is one division of Merrill Lynch
paying another division the 17 million or

01:18:24.449 --> 01:18:26.070
whatever it adds —
David Maisel: Oh, that's a good question.

01:18:26.070 --> 01:18:27.070
No.

01:18:27.070 --> 01:18:31.070
If they got stuck with the whole loan, then
they're sort of paying themselves, I guess.

01:18:31.070 --> 01:18:34.040
Yeah, I didn't think about it that way.

01:18:34.040 --> 01:18:35.040
Yeah.

01:18:35.040 --> 01:18:36.040
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:18:36.040 --> 01:18:37.040
Okay.

01:18:37.040 --> 01:18:38.040
Wow.

01:18:38.040 --> 01:18:39.040
What a world.

01:18:39.040 --> 01:18:40.040
David Maisel: I'm not sure there was a risk
of that when it happened, because most of

01:18:40.040 --> 01:18:41.040
it was insured.

01:18:41.040 --> 01:18:42.040
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:18:42.040 --> 01:18:45.370
This is a world that is largely foreign to
me.

01:18:45.370 --> 01:18:52.690
But let's come back to, as you mentioned,
the other very, very critical kind of leg

01:18:52.690 --> 01:18:54.620
on the stool, which is a distribution deal.

01:18:54.620 --> 01:18:55.620
David Maisel: Yeah.

01:18:55.620 --> 01:18:58.590
Tim Ferriss: How did you go about securing
that?

01:18:58.590 --> 01:19:04.321
How did you go about trying to pick, is all
distribution more or less the same thing and

01:19:04.321 --> 01:19:08.050
it's just a different marquee name on the
same function and they kind of all performed

01:19:08.050 --> 01:19:09.239
the same way?

01:19:09.239 --> 01:19:10.920
How did you go about navigating that?

01:19:10.920 --> 01:19:14.230
David Maisel: Well, it was easy because we
didn't have a choice to pick.

01:19:14.230 --> 01:19:15.719
Nobody wanted to be our partner.

01:19:15.719 --> 01:19:23.840
So at the end of the day, only one person
said yes, which was Paramount, part of Viacom.

01:19:23.840 --> 01:19:26.730
But the way we got there, I mean, no, at the
time, this is really important.

01:19:26.730 --> 01:19:31.210
In 2003, 2004, 2005, Marvel wasn't the Marvel
it is today.

01:19:31.210 --> 01:19:38.520
So everyone that's listening, it's many people
probably don't remember that Marvel wasn't

01:19:38.520 --> 01:19:41.949
this huge thing that it's been the past 15
years.

01:19:41.949 --> 01:19:46.440
And in Hollywood, I mean, if it was, by the
way, I never would've gotten this opportunity.

01:19:46.440 --> 01:19:51.280
So Disney or somebody else could have bought
Marvel for 500 million much earlier if they

01:19:51.280 --> 01:19:52.280
wanted to.

01:19:52.280 --> 01:19:56.380
And so the studios, nobody wanted to distribute
these movies.

01:19:56.380 --> 01:19:57.960
And it wasn't just Sony.

01:19:57.960 --> 01:20:01.650
It was Universal and others, we had obstacles.

01:20:01.650 --> 01:20:08.940
Now we wanted great terms, but nobody really
believed in these properties.

01:20:08.940 --> 01:20:13.731
And Iron Man, New Line, which is part of Warner
Bros., they had the rights for many, many

01:20:13.731 --> 01:20:18.150
years and I think for seven or eight years
and never thought it was worthy to make into

01:20:18.150 --> 01:20:21.560
a film, and they let the rights expire and
gave it back to us.

01:20:21.560 --> 01:20:24.090
So it was a very different world.

01:20:24.090 --> 01:20:26.500
So why Paramount?

01:20:26.500 --> 01:20:32.360
When I was at Disney, I got to be friendly
with a guy named Rob Moore, who was one of

01:20:32.360 --> 01:20:34.890
the senior people at the studio at Disney.

01:20:34.890 --> 01:20:38.000
He was Vice Chairman of Paramount at this
time.

01:20:38.000 --> 01:20:41.750
He was on his way to a Lakers game, and I
tackled him in the parking lot and just bugged

01:20:41.750 --> 01:20:46.450
him enough because he didn't want to be late
to the basketball game that he agreed to.

01:20:46.450 --> 01:20:51.440
Seriously, he agreed to have a meeting with
me.

01:20:51.440 --> 01:20:54.570
And then Brad Gray was his boss, who Avi Arad
knew.

01:20:54.570 --> 01:20:59.790
And so we sort of tag-teamed these two men
and convinced them that this was a good bet.

01:20:59.790 --> 01:21:02.420
And to your point, we showed them that they
were the last money.

01:21:02.420 --> 01:21:04.000
They put up the last 150 million.

01:21:04.000 --> 01:21:06.280
They got the first 150 million back.

01:21:06.280 --> 01:21:11.300
So the film only had to do a percentage of
X-Men for them to at least get their money

01:21:11.300 --> 01:21:12.300
back.

01:21:12.300 --> 01:21:15.570
Tim Ferriss: So they were sort of last in,
first out —

01:21:15.570 --> 01:21:16.570
David Maisel: They were, yeah.

01:21:16.570 --> 01:21:17.570
Tim Ferriss: — in terms of preferences.

01:21:17.570 --> 01:21:18.570
David Maisel: Yes.

01:21:18.570 --> 01:21:19.570
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah.

01:21:19.570 --> 01:21:21.840
David Maisel: But even with that, nobody else
wanted to do it.

01:21:21.840 --> 01:21:22.840
Yeah.

01:21:22.840 --> 01:21:28.711
Tim Ferriss: Aside from getting slide tackled
in the Lakers parking lot, what was it, do

01:21:28.711 --> 01:21:34.030
you think, and maybe you already answered
this, and I just am too dense and it didn't

01:21:34.030 --> 01:21:36.020
land.

01:21:36.020 --> 01:21:40.610
What led them to say yes in contrast to all
the others who said no?

01:21:40.610 --> 01:21:47.140
What did they see or how did you refine the
pitch that didn't exist in the other conversations?

01:21:47.140 --> 01:21:55.900
David Maisel: I think they listened, and Rob
trusted me from our times at Disney.

01:21:55.900 --> 01:21:58.489
He knew me pretty well, so the past relationship
helped.

01:21:58.489 --> 01:22:03.719
And he knew I wasn't careless with my analysis
or my statements.

01:22:03.719 --> 01:22:11.400
And so at least for Rob, I can't speak for
Brad, but for Rob, he understood.

01:22:11.400 --> 01:22:15.560
And when he finally listened to where the
break even was for them, he saw it was a good

01:22:15.560 --> 01:22:17.230
bet, right?

01:22:17.230 --> 01:22:22.969
In the upside, he had distribution rights
to, I think five or 10 movies in the downside.

01:22:22.969 --> 01:22:26.840
He basically broke even if the film didn't
do well.

01:22:26.840 --> 01:22:30.130
There's a lot of other games.

01:22:30.130 --> 01:22:34.320
Studios make money internally even on P&amp;A.

01:22:34.320 --> 01:22:36.850
Tim Ferriss: Could you just explain P&amp;A?

01:22:36.850 --> 01:22:39.250
David Maisel: Print and advertising.

01:22:39.250 --> 01:22:44.270
So I remember Jeffrey Katzenberg had a distribution
deal with Paramount, and he was running DreamWorks

01:22:44.270 --> 01:22:48.910
Animation and he basically coached me on what
terms I should ask for and mentored me on

01:22:48.910 --> 01:22:49.910
that.

01:22:49.910 --> 01:22:51.710
And so we had a deal that was really good.

01:22:51.710 --> 01:22:53.840
There was no non-cash charges.

01:22:53.840 --> 01:22:55.380
There was no overhead charge to us.

01:22:55.380 --> 01:22:57.010
Tim Ferriss: What is a non-cash charge?

01:22:57.010 --> 01:22:59.102
David Maisel: Yeah, that's a good question.

01:22:59.102 --> 01:23:00.690
Tim Ferriss: I mean, I understand the wording,
but I don't know what it is.

01:23:00.690 --> 01:23:04.290
David Maisel: They would inflate the costs
that they got reimbursed.

01:23:04.290 --> 01:23:09.250
So they would say they spent 150 million on
marketing, but 20 million of that's overhead

01:23:09.250 --> 01:23:11.870
and another 10 million some odd costs, so
—

01:23:11.870 --> 01:23:14.100
Tim Ferriss: It's shipping and handling for
infomercial products.

01:23:14.100 --> 01:23:15.980
You're like, "How is shipping and handling
$17?"

01:23:15.980 --> 01:23:16.980
David Maisel: Yeah.

01:23:16.980 --> 01:23:17.980
Tim Ferriss: Right.

01:23:17.980 --> 01:23:18.980
Yeah, extra handling, man.

01:23:18.980 --> 01:23:19.980
Yeah, right.

01:23:19.980 --> 01:23:20.980
Okay.

01:23:20.980 --> 01:23:24.520
David Maisel: It actually was important because
it can eat up all your profits on a movie

01:23:24.520 --> 01:23:26.440
if you get charged too much.

01:23:26.440 --> 01:23:30.480
We had a 10 percent distribution fee, which
is really attractive as well.

01:23:30.480 --> 01:23:34.840
I think George Lucas had the lowest I'm aware
of at six or seven percent for Star Wars.

01:23:34.840 --> 01:23:37.400
Tim Ferriss: So how the hell did he get that?

01:23:37.400 --> 01:23:38.600
I mean, that was also at the time.

01:23:38.600 --> 01:23:43.900
It's easy for people to forget, but it's not
like that was the juggernaut franchise that

01:23:43.900 --> 01:23:44.900
it is today.

01:23:44.900 --> 01:23:47.870
I don't want to take us too far afield, but
wow.

01:23:47.870 --> 01:23:48.870
Okay.

01:23:48.870 --> 01:23:49.870
Got it.

01:23:49.870 --> 01:23:58.770
So they say, all right, worst case we recoup
best case we have rights to these things you

01:23:58.770 --> 01:24:03.220
claim will be as predictable or similarly
predictable to sequels.

01:24:03.220 --> 01:24:04.220
David Maisel: Sure.

01:24:04.220 --> 01:24:05.220
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:24:05.220 --> 01:24:09.400
David Maisel: And Tim, the part that I guess
with both the bankers and with Paramount is

01:24:09.400 --> 01:24:15.360
I would strongly make the case creatively
for why I thought Iron Man would be a success

01:24:15.360 --> 01:24:21.090
and why the MCU, which wasn't called at the
time, could be a success.

01:24:21.090 --> 01:24:25.870
And then also Avi and Kevin would come into
these meetings with the bankers and with the

01:24:25.870 --> 01:24:31.710
distribution partners to talk about their
experiences on those movies of X-Men and Spider-Man.

01:24:31.710 --> 01:24:39.060
So we had the sexy halo effect of producers
on those movies, even though we weren't actually

01:24:39.060 --> 01:24:45.230
full producers and all those things, especially
with the bankers were really important.

01:24:45.230 --> 01:24:54.170
And with Rob and Paramount, I think it was
more the financial trade for them, but they

01:24:54.170 --> 01:24:59.251
saw the success of Spider-Man with Sony and
hoped that they could get that upside, which

01:24:59.251 --> 01:25:00.251
they did.

01:25:00.251 --> 01:25:01.251
Tim Ferriss: So why Iron Man?

01:25:01.251 --> 01:25:02.251
Right?

01:25:02.251 --> 01:25:03.251
As you know, we've talked about this.

01:25:03.251 --> 01:25:04.880
I love comics.

01:25:04.880 --> 01:25:11.530
I have boxes and boxes and boxes of comic
books from my childhood, still cardboard-backed

01:25:11.530 --> 01:25:19.190
and poly-bagged sitting in my parents' probably
garage being eaten by mice or something at

01:25:19.190 --> 01:25:20.510
this point.

01:25:20.510 --> 01:25:24.280
But there are a lot of characters to choose
from.

01:25:24.280 --> 01:25:26.040
You have a lot of options.

01:25:26.040 --> 01:25:27.040
Why Iron Man?

01:25:27.040 --> 01:25:29.420
David Maisel: We didn't have that many options.

01:25:29.420 --> 01:25:33.430
Again, so when we closed the financing, we
didn't have Iron Man.

01:25:33.430 --> 01:25:40.750
We listed 10 characters, and those 10 characters
were led by Captain America, Thor, this thing

01:25:40.750 --> 01:25:47.110
called Avengers, which people thought was
a British spy TV show.

01:25:47.110 --> 01:25:49.520
And The Avengers weren't defined in our deal.

01:25:49.520 --> 01:25:54.310
So it could be, as with the comics, different
characters in The Avengers, right?

01:25:54.310 --> 01:25:58.020
So it was sort of a weird category of a property.

01:25:58.020 --> 01:25:59.300
There was Black Panther in there.

01:25:59.300 --> 01:26:02.040
There was Doctor Strange, but nobody cared
about those characters at all.

01:26:02.040 --> 01:26:05.900
That people thought of Black Panther as a
terrorist group from the '60s.

01:26:05.900 --> 01:26:07.730
Doctor Strange was a small comic.

01:26:07.730 --> 01:26:14.330
I think we had Cloak and Dagger in there,
Shang-Chi, which finally made it as a movie,

01:26:14.330 --> 01:26:17.040
Power Pack, that people don't know these names.

01:26:17.040 --> 01:26:22.840
I mean, there weren't that many big names,
and I'm not sure we even put Thor in the beginning

01:26:22.840 --> 01:26:27.489
because there was disagreement internally
whether Thor was even practical, given the

01:26:27.489 --> 01:26:29.880
cost of Asgard.

01:26:29.880 --> 01:26:33.670
And so for a while, we didn't know what the
first movie was going to be.

01:26:33.670 --> 01:26:35.770
I was able to get the rights for Hulk back.

01:26:35.770 --> 01:26:41.280
I mentioned Ron Meyer went to become chairman
of Universal from CAA earlier.

01:26:41.280 --> 01:26:48.830
And so Ron, I knew very well, and I texted
him and asked him for a meeting, and they

01:26:48.830 --> 01:26:55.110
had done the first Hulk movie with Ang Lee,
which was critically well received, but not

01:26:55.110 --> 01:26:56.480
box office-wise.

01:26:56.480 --> 01:26:57.949
And they were owned by GE at the time.

01:26:57.949 --> 01:27:00.290
And I said, "Ron, are you ever going to make
a Hulk movie?"

01:27:00.290 --> 01:27:02.810
And he said, "No, probably not."

01:27:02.810 --> 01:27:08.219
And I said, "What about if I finance it and
you distribute it, I'll give you 10 percent?"

01:27:08.219 --> 01:27:09.370
And he said, "Yes."

01:27:09.370 --> 01:27:15.040
And so that's why we got the Hulk back into
the Marvel Universe.

01:27:15.040 --> 01:27:19.900
And that's why the first Hulk movie was made
in 2008, because by making one film, we got

01:27:19.900 --> 01:27:26.389
the rights back for Hulk in perpetuity and
at least as a supporting character.

01:27:26.389 --> 01:27:32.270
And also, we were splitting toys 50/50 with
Universal, and that became way in our favor.

01:27:32.270 --> 01:27:36.600
And Hulk Hands were our number one toy, so
we were thinking of Hulk potentially as the

01:27:36.600 --> 01:27:38.469
first movie.

01:27:38.469 --> 01:27:43.330
And then Iron Man came back and Kevin and
I were both really excited about Iron Man,

01:27:43.330 --> 01:27:45.350
as was Avi Arad who was still at the studio.

01:27:45.350 --> 01:27:47.610
He left in early 2006.

01:27:47.610 --> 01:27:50.780
And so we zeroed in on that.

01:27:50.780 --> 01:27:52.940
I don't know exactly why other people missed
it.

01:27:52.940 --> 01:27:55.960
I think what we were excited about was the
man inside the suit.

01:27:55.960 --> 01:28:02.699
We love the sexy suit of armor like most people
do, but Tony Stark is such a cool character.

01:28:02.699 --> 01:28:07.520
And if you don't see Tony Stark, you don't
see how good the movie can be.

01:28:07.520 --> 01:28:09.929
And so I think that was probably the breakthrough
for the three of us.

01:28:09.929 --> 01:28:16.630
Tim Ferriss: I can't remember which film it
was from, but there's one of the Marvel films.

01:28:16.630 --> 01:28:21.389
I think it was quite a bit later where there's
some disagreement, I want to say it's between

01:28:21.389 --> 01:28:22.800
Thor and Stark.

01:28:22.800 --> 01:28:27.489
And he's like, "Well, if we take that suit
off you, who are you?"

01:28:27.489 --> 01:28:30.139
And he's like, "Billionaire, playboy…"

01:28:30.139 --> 01:28:31.730
David Maisel: Exactly.

01:28:31.730 --> 01:28:33.850
Tim Ferriss: Oh, God.

01:28:33.850 --> 01:28:35.449
What a character.

01:28:35.449 --> 01:28:42.389
Let's then walk into the next steps for Iron
Man.

01:28:42.389 --> 01:28:50.260
Because I feel like this is such a, maybe
not make it or break it, but such a critical

01:28:50.260 --> 01:28:59.230
project overall as a harbinger of things to
come perhaps, as a signal, as a proof-of-concept.

01:28:59.230 --> 01:29:06.580
What were some of the key decisions or critical
moments in that development phase and then

01:29:06.580 --> 01:29:08.380
moving into the theatrical release?

01:29:08.380 --> 01:29:13.070
David Maisel: To give perspective on that,
we talked about how in 2004, 2005, it was

01:29:13.070 --> 01:29:19.250
primarily raising the money and getting a
distribution partner in Paramount.

01:29:19.250 --> 01:29:25.690
I was then promoted to vice chairman of the
studio in that period.

01:29:25.690 --> 01:29:29.560
And in 2006, Avi left the studio in early
2006.

01:29:29.560 --> 01:29:36.040
And then I was running the studio myself and
I promoted Kevin Feige, who was a young kid

01:29:36.040 --> 01:29:41.040
that was helping Avi on script notes who I'd
gotten to know very well and admired him,

01:29:41.040 --> 01:29:43.830
his creativity and his love of the comics.

01:29:43.830 --> 01:29:48.340
I promoted him to be my president of production
and I was chairman of the studio and promoted

01:29:48.340 --> 01:29:49.670
to that.

01:29:49.670 --> 01:29:56.410
I also was fortunate to be promoted to office
of the CEO with Ike and John of the parent

01:29:56.410 --> 01:29:57.949
company.

01:29:57.949 --> 01:30:04.790
And so it's relevant because with those pieces
in place, we were able to focus solely on

01:30:04.790 --> 01:30:06.630
making that film.

01:30:06.630 --> 01:30:09.520
There was no more internal politics.

01:30:09.520 --> 01:30:14.750
There wasn't these external things we needed
like financing or distribution deals.

01:30:14.750 --> 01:30:15.940
We had that.

01:30:15.940 --> 01:30:17.460
We had the rights back.

01:30:17.460 --> 01:30:20.960
We had a clean structure in the company.

01:30:20.960 --> 01:30:25.500
I promoted my ex-assistant into the creative
realm.

01:30:25.500 --> 01:30:28.670
He became our number two guy creatively, Jeremy
Latcham.

01:30:28.670 --> 01:30:35.070
We hired Louis D'Esposito and Victoria Alonso,
two amazing talents, early in that period

01:30:35.070 --> 01:30:36.070
of time.

01:30:36.070 --> 01:30:39.980
And all of us then focused on making this
movie.

01:30:39.980 --> 01:30:45.130
And Jon Favreau was identified as the director
and Robert Downey as the actor, that was the

01:30:45.130 --> 01:30:46.239
two big decisions.

01:30:46.239 --> 01:30:48.003
Tim Ferriss: How did those two two happen?

01:30:48.003 --> 01:30:49.003
How did those two end up happening?

01:30:49.003 --> 01:30:53.310
David Maisel: A lot of people think of Jon
today for his Star Wars stuff and a lot of

01:30:53.310 --> 01:30:59.960
visual effects, but at the time he was a lot
more known for Swingers and for those types

01:30:59.960 --> 01:31:00.960
of films.

01:31:00.960 --> 01:31:01.960
Tim Ferriss: People forget about Elf too.

01:31:01.960 --> 01:31:03.120
David Maisel: Yeah.

01:31:03.120 --> 01:31:09.110
And he was just coming off of Zathura and
we knew — we felt like we knew how to do

01:31:09.110 --> 01:31:13.610
the comic book part of things, like the action
and the comic booky part of stuff.

01:31:13.610 --> 01:31:16.510
But we also knew we didn't have a lot of money
to make the movie.

01:31:16.510 --> 01:31:22.920
I could greenlight to $165 million, but I
told my board I'd make the film at a budget

01:31:22.920 --> 01:31:28.460
that would allow it to break even at a percentage,
I think two-thirds of X-Men.

01:31:28.460 --> 01:31:32.770
And so my budget for the film was about 100,
$105 million.

01:31:32.770 --> 01:31:35.070
Now that's — sounds like a lot, but it wasn't.

01:31:35.070 --> 01:31:38.580
I mean, Batman that year was 230 million.

01:31:38.580 --> 01:31:43.040
And so that allowed us to have some action,
but not a lot.

01:31:43.040 --> 01:31:48.219
So we knew we'd have an hour and 45 minutes
of Tony Stark in his workshop or talking to

01:31:48.219 --> 01:31:50.000
Pepper Potts.

01:31:50.000 --> 01:31:55.409
And Jon was great on those scenes and is great
on those scenes, and also getting the best

01:31:55.409 --> 01:31:57.580
out of talent like Robert Downey.

01:31:57.580 --> 01:32:02.390
I think that was something we all saw in Favreau.

01:32:02.390 --> 01:32:05.400
Plus, he seemed very committed to it.

01:32:05.400 --> 01:32:11.900
His career obviously has taken off in the
past 15 years tremendously, and he's shown

01:32:11.900 --> 01:32:15.400
his talent in so many other ways.

01:32:15.400 --> 01:32:20.840
And Robert Downey, I think, originally came
from John and Kevin.

01:32:20.840 --> 01:32:24.920
They both were proponents for Robert as studio
chairman.

01:32:24.920 --> 01:32:31.219
I had to give the final thumbs up and thumbs
down on that, but I agreed with them.

01:32:31.219 --> 01:32:35.610
Knowing Tony Stark from the comics and Robert's
work, it wasn't obvious.

01:32:35.610 --> 01:32:37.280
Robert had never had a box office success.

01:32:37.280 --> 01:32:43.140
He had just been arrested a couple years earlier
for drugs and spent time in jail, so it's

01:32:43.140 --> 01:32:45.280
not who you bet your company on.

01:32:45.280 --> 01:32:50.380
And he came in and did a video audition, which
is also rare.

01:32:50.380 --> 01:32:54.790
And I remember taking that recommendation
to Ike and the board and showing them that

01:32:54.790 --> 01:33:02.960
video audition and they asked a lot of tough
questions, but they also agreed.

01:33:02.960 --> 01:33:04.630
That was a key part.

01:33:04.630 --> 01:33:10.469
I think with those two pieces in place, Kevin
and I were off to the races with Jon and Robert

01:33:10.469 --> 01:33:11.560
to make this film.

01:33:11.560 --> 01:33:15.699
Which is, I think this month, it's our 15th
anniversary of release.

01:33:15.699 --> 01:33:19.180
Tim Ferriss: What were some of the biggest
challenges?

01:33:19.180 --> 01:33:25.469
It could have been in production or in distribution
or in any facet of that experience.

01:33:25.469 --> 01:33:28.500
David Maisel: What I just talked you through
was the biggest, Tim.

01:33:28.500 --> 01:33:34.760
It was getting the job at Marvel, getting
the financing in place, convincing the board,

01:33:34.760 --> 01:33:39.740
getting the distribution deal, getting the
rights back to Iron Man, getting the board

01:33:39.740 --> 01:33:44.750
to agree with us on Robert Downey.

01:33:44.750 --> 01:33:49.380
And getting the internal politics of Marvel,
like there are in every company, settled so

01:33:49.380 --> 01:33:55.170
by that spring of 2006, two years before the
movie comes out, we have all those pieces

01:33:55.170 --> 01:33:56.199
in place.

01:33:56.199 --> 01:33:58.480
We didn't have an office.

01:33:58.480 --> 01:34:03.200
We took the Mercedes-Benz dealership on Santa
Monica Boulevard.

01:34:03.200 --> 01:34:08.080
Playboy had an office in the very top corner
and they were having financial difficulties.

01:34:08.080 --> 01:34:16.230
And I sublet it from Hefner and put a Marvel
Studio sign outside that cost $10,000 and

01:34:16.230 --> 01:34:22.250
we announced our presence and we just would
work there 18 hours a day.

01:34:22.250 --> 01:34:26.581
We moved pre-production for a movie, which
is where all the planning gets done, right

01:34:26.581 --> 01:34:31.170
there in that same floor, which is normally
never done with a studio, which was Kevin

01:34:31.170 --> 01:34:32.890
and I.

01:34:32.890 --> 01:34:36.660
But I guess there's another thing that people
should hear about is, I had this philosophy

01:34:36.660 --> 01:34:44.340
that studios, Hollywood studios, were — had
this unnecessary layer of management called

01:34:44.340 --> 01:34:47.690
studio executives who hire the producers.

01:34:47.690 --> 01:34:51.190
So our model was, there's no studio executives.

01:34:51.190 --> 01:34:53.090
We were the producers of the movie.

01:34:53.090 --> 01:34:58.780
And so Kevin and I were technically studio
executives, but we were working on the film.

01:34:58.780 --> 01:34:59.960
Kevin, every day.

01:34:59.960 --> 01:35:04.820
Me with a significant chunk of my time because
I was also co-CEO, and we didn't have that

01:35:04.820 --> 01:35:05.820
layer.

01:35:05.820 --> 01:35:11.320
So we all, with Jon and Robert and all the
pre-production, were all together for months

01:35:11.320 --> 01:35:12.840
planning out this film.

01:35:12.840 --> 01:35:18.570
We had a pretty smooth ride, amazingly, from
that point on.

01:35:18.570 --> 01:35:23.420
We took the Spruce Goose hangar by the ocean
where Howard Hughes had this huge wooden hangar

01:35:23.420 --> 01:35:25.949
that he built the biggest airplane in the
world.

01:35:25.949 --> 01:35:32.710
And that's appropriately where we filmed it
since Tony Stark is influenced by Howard Hughes.

01:35:32.710 --> 01:35:37.060
And that was a big decision because we didn't
get tax incentives by being in California.

01:35:37.060 --> 01:35:45.840
But it turned out to be great because we were
all so close and it became a set of people

01:35:45.840 --> 01:35:47.030
that were really enthusiastic.

01:35:47.030 --> 01:35:49.030
Gwyneth Paltrow joined.

01:35:49.030 --> 01:35:50.270
That was a big win.

01:35:50.270 --> 01:35:54.440
I think it was Bryan Lourd who called me and
said Gwyneth was interested.

01:35:54.440 --> 01:35:59.340
And my God, to get an Academy Award-winning
actress like her was a dream.

01:35:59.340 --> 01:36:02.889
So it all came together.

01:36:02.889 --> 01:36:08.970
We had some disagreements at the end about
re-shoots, which cost some money.

01:36:08.970 --> 01:36:14.420
But I was able to get Ike and the board to
approve the ones that we wanted to do.

01:36:14.420 --> 01:36:19.199
Marketing was a tough challenge too, to get
Paramount to manage marketing because all

01:36:19.199 --> 01:36:24.040
this stuff doesn't make any sense if nobody
goes to see the movie.

01:36:24.040 --> 01:36:29.710
I think it was Katzenberg who told me to show
up at every meeting and that make sure we

01:36:29.710 --> 01:36:32.000
get a good release date.

01:36:32.000 --> 01:36:35.860
In retrospect, we chose May 2nd because nobody
else wanted it.

01:36:35.860 --> 01:36:38.520
It was an early date.

01:36:38.520 --> 01:36:41.060
People thought the summer started with Memorial
Day.

01:36:41.060 --> 01:36:45.580
And so us going to May 2nd, now it's a great
date and Marvel has it every year.

01:36:45.580 --> 01:36:52.230
But it turned out to be really good because
we had the first movie of the summer and Paramount

01:36:52.230 --> 01:36:53.230
was — gave us that date.

01:36:53.230 --> 01:36:57.230
They keep their best dates for their own movies,
so we were able to get a date they didn't

01:36:57.230 --> 01:36:59.090
want.

01:36:59.090 --> 01:37:03.360
It sounds weird to say things went smoothly
from that point on, but for those two years,

01:37:03.360 --> 01:37:04.410
things went very smoothly.

01:37:04.410 --> 01:37:08.920
Tim Ferriss: Let me rewind just a little bit
because I have some notes in front of me that

01:37:08.920 --> 01:37:11.250
I want to fact check with you.

01:37:11.250 --> 01:37:15.860
And you can't believe everything you read
on the internet, but this is Hollywood Reporter,

01:37:15.860 --> 01:37:18.860
so you would hope that it is accurate.

01:37:18.860 --> 01:37:23.550
Is it true that you had an early proof-of-concept
in the sense of changing Marvel's strategy

01:37:23.550 --> 01:37:28.230
with a low-budget Iron Man animated film,
or maybe animatics?

01:37:28.230 --> 01:37:29.230
Is that true?

01:37:29.230 --> 01:37:30.420
David Maisel: Yeah, it is.

01:37:30.420 --> 01:37:35.820
In 2004, while I was raising the money for
the theatrical films, I wanted to prove to

01:37:35.820 --> 01:37:39.230
the board that we actually could produce something.

01:37:39.230 --> 01:37:43.270
And so I was able to make a deal with Lionsgate,
Michael Burns and Jon Feltheimer who still

01:37:43.270 --> 01:37:48.440
run Lionsgate, that we'd make $3 million direct-to-DVD
animated movies.

01:37:48.440 --> 01:37:50.730
I think the budget was three to four million.

01:37:50.730 --> 01:37:53.110
And they put up the money.

01:37:53.110 --> 01:37:56.920
We at Marvel Studios produced it.

01:37:56.920 --> 01:37:58.070
We hired a guy named Eric Rollman.

01:37:58.070 --> 01:38:01.219
Tim Ferriss: For those who aren't watching
the video, that was air quotes in that example.

01:38:01.219 --> 01:38:04.170
David Maisel: And then I remember they got
their three or four million back and we split

01:38:04.170 --> 01:38:05.170
the money.

01:38:05.170 --> 01:38:06.170
So we had no risk.

01:38:06.170 --> 01:38:08.719
It was like a mini Marvel Studios type of
deal.

01:38:08.719 --> 01:38:10.730
I forget the order.

01:38:10.730 --> 01:38:12.360
We made a Doctor Strange one.

01:38:12.360 --> 01:38:13.360
We made Iron Man.

01:38:13.360 --> 01:38:14.360
We made Avengers.

01:38:14.360 --> 01:38:15.460
We made a Hulk one.

01:38:15.460 --> 01:38:16.590
And they all did pretty well.

01:38:16.590 --> 01:38:18.530
And so they're still fun to watch.

01:38:18.530 --> 01:38:24.060
And that was our chance to creatively be in
charge of a project and show the board that

01:38:24.060 --> 01:38:25.090
we could do it.

01:38:25.090 --> 01:38:26.489
Tim Ferriss: Incredible.

01:38:26.489 --> 01:38:28.849
David Maisel: A lot of people don't point
that out, Tim.

01:38:28.849 --> 01:38:29.849
You do good research.

01:38:29.849 --> 01:38:36.170
Tim Ferriss: Well, I need to thank my team
also for finding this particular example.

01:38:36.170 --> 01:38:45.650
I love finding these proof-of-concept moments
or talking as we have been also about structuring.

01:38:45.650 --> 01:38:51.250
Because as you mentioned, I think in many
worlds, not just film, not just Hollywood,

01:38:51.250 --> 01:38:58.560
there is almost a religious divide between
the people who are derisively called suits

01:38:58.560 --> 01:39:03.889
and the people who are sometimes over-romanticized
as creatives.

01:39:03.889 --> 01:39:13.300
And you can be a one-event athlete in that
sense that you stick really narrowly within

01:39:13.300 --> 01:39:17.369
a lane and you can become world-class and
very successful doing that.

01:39:17.369 --> 01:39:22.290
But in many cases, it's like the creatives
don't get to do what they would most want

01:39:22.290 --> 01:39:30.469
to do unless things are structured by someone
who understands incentives and these various

01:39:30.469 --> 01:39:31.469
provisions.

01:39:31.469 --> 01:39:34.770
So I find your story so compelling for a lot
of reasons.

01:39:34.770 --> 01:39:41.810
One of which is, you blend these capacities
in a very effective way because they could

01:39:41.810 --> 01:39:43.050
be blended ineffectively, right?

01:39:43.050 --> 01:39:48.820
David Maisel: No, and part of it, Tim, is
I think most people, because I haven't done

01:39:48.820 --> 01:39:57.060
press, they view my role as very much the
financial structuring, the distribution deal,

01:39:57.060 --> 01:40:02.650
the co-CEO role on Marvel, and less on the
creative side.

01:40:02.650 --> 01:40:04.449
You know what?

01:40:04.449 --> 01:40:07.690
In retrospect, in talking this through with
you, the biggest creative thing I've ever

01:40:07.690 --> 01:40:11.760
done in my life was the first — that weekend
and having the vision for the MCU.

01:40:11.760 --> 01:40:17.980
For having what became the Universe and just
intuitively believing these characters could

01:40:17.980 --> 01:40:20.750
become a modern mythology for this world.

01:40:20.750 --> 01:40:24.630
And that's a delusional belief I had at the
time, in retrospect.

01:40:24.630 --> 01:40:27.500
I didn't realize the bar of this.

01:40:27.500 --> 01:40:33.290
And then I had to put on the hat that I had
to do for each of the dominoes.

01:40:33.290 --> 01:40:38.739
So if I went into my board and I just creatively
talked, they wouldn't have listened to me.

01:40:38.739 --> 01:40:44.630
So I had to argue in their language about
finance and to the bankers about the financing

01:40:44.630 --> 01:40:48.790
and to Paramount about their break-evens.

01:40:48.790 --> 01:40:55.620
And politically structure a studio in terms
of personnel that I thought could do well.

01:40:55.620 --> 01:41:01.340
And then to manage creative people like Jon
Favreau and Robert Downey and Kevin Feige,

01:41:01.340 --> 01:41:02.810
my president of production.

01:41:02.810 --> 01:41:04.409
Who, they all worked for me.

01:41:04.409 --> 01:41:08.030
I was chairman of the studio.

01:41:08.030 --> 01:41:14.260
But to get the best out of them, I learned
this from my time at CAA and Disney, it can't

01:41:14.260 --> 01:41:15.880
be because you're their boss.

01:41:15.880 --> 01:41:23.730
It's got to be because you give them the creative
freedom to explore and be careful about guardrails.

01:41:23.730 --> 01:41:29.810
So a lot of people would say, "Oh, it was
my idea, I was studio chairman, to do Robert

01:41:29.810 --> 01:41:30.810
Downey."

01:41:30.810 --> 01:41:34.710
Rather than saying, "John and Kevin recommended
him."

01:41:34.710 --> 01:41:36.600
I love that.

01:41:36.600 --> 01:41:37.850
That's why you hire good people.

01:41:37.850 --> 01:41:40.380
That's why I'm very proud of promoting Kevin
Feige.

01:41:40.380 --> 01:41:44.880
A lot of people, I think, believe because
I haven't been doing press that Stan Lee or

01:41:44.880 --> 01:41:49.310
Kevin created Marvel Studios.

01:41:49.310 --> 01:41:55.560
And as we heard in the story, that that's
not the case.

01:41:55.560 --> 01:41:59.440
The baton came to me and that was my legacy,
which I'm really proud of.

01:41:59.440 --> 01:42:01.960
But I'm also really proud of identifying Kevin.

01:42:01.960 --> 01:42:07.250
And I remember the conversation with Ike,
who had never met Kevin before, in 2006.

01:42:07.250 --> 01:42:13.220
Ike's in New York and Kevin and I were in
L.A., "You need to hire somebody," when Avi

01:42:13.220 --> 01:42:14.220
left.

01:42:14.220 --> 01:42:15.790
I said, "No, Kevin can do it.

01:42:15.790 --> 01:42:18.420
He can be my president in production."

01:42:18.420 --> 01:42:22.040
When I sold the company to Disney, I told
the same thing to Iger that Kevin could run

01:42:22.040 --> 01:42:24.290
it.

01:42:24.290 --> 01:42:31.250
I'm his biggest fan in how he's done the films
the past 10 years and has become the most

01:42:31.250 --> 01:42:34.000
respected creative producer in Hollywood.

01:42:34.000 --> 01:42:40.580
And so part of managing creativity is knowing
when to exert yours and when to get the best

01:42:40.580 --> 01:42:42.090
out from others.

01:42:42.090 --> 01:42:49.040
I remember the biggest time I ever exerted
mine was early in 2006.

01:42:49.040 --> 01:42:56.250
I think it was over Christmas holidays, I
got unhappy with the Iron Man script.

01:42:56.250 --> 01:43:03.270
And the script at the time, Jeff Bridges is
Obadiah Stane, who, in the movie is the villain,

01:43:03.270 --> 01:43:06.810
was actually working for The Mandarin.

01:43:06.810 --> 01:43:12.920
And so there was a whole 'nother scene or
two where we met The Mandarin in China and

01:43:12.920 --> 01:43:17.420
he was manipulating Obadiah Stane and there
was another big action sequence.

01:43:17.420 --> 01:43:22.900
And that felt too comic booky to me because,
for these movies to work, I was running marketing

01:43:22.900 --> 01:43:24.000
from Marvel's perspective.

01:43:24.000 --> 01:43:25.880
I didn't delegate that.

01:43:25.880 --> 01:43:28.960
I was going to every meeting as the chairman
of Marvel Studios, I had the relationship

01:43:28.960 --> 01:43:31.900
with Rob Moore to get the best out of Paramount.

01:43:31.900 --> 01:43:37.000
Kevin was on set every day and so we would
talk about everything, of course.

01:43:37.000 --> 01:43:40.690
And I'd go to set many times and Kevin would
come to marketing meetings.

01:43:40.690 --> 01:43:45.590
But we needed to appeal from a marketing point
of view to non-comic book fans.

01:43:45.590 --> 01:43:47.580
Otherwise, we would have died.

01:43:47.580 --> 01:43:50.060
Only four or five thousand people read Iron
Man comics.

01:43:50.060 --> 01:43:55.150
So I needed the movie to be appealing to my
mom, my girlfriend at the time, the whole

01:43:55.150 --> 01:43:56.150
world.

01:43:56.150 --> 01:44:00.340
And so it had to be a classic story about
a man changing his life who happened to change

01:44:00.340 --> 01:44:04.270
into being the superhero and making other
big decisions.

01:44:04.270 --> 01:44:10.710
And so my Spidey sense was the story was getting
too comic booky in act three.

01:44:10.710 --> 01:44:14.849
And we needed to make it more Shakespearean
where the father figure of Obadiah was the

01:44:14.849 --> 01:44:15.909
villain.

01:44:15.909 --> 01:44:21.730
So I went in as studio chairman where I could
have dictated that and told Jon Favreau and

01:44:21.730 --> 01:44:25.460
Kevin, "We're chopping off that part of the
movie."

01:44:25.460 --> 01:44:30.940
But instead I went in, I remember first calling
Ike and Jon and telling them what I thought

01:44:30.940 --> 01:44:35.830
about and my discomfort, and then going in
and calling a meeting and recommending it

01:44:35.830 --> 01:44:45.090
and explaining why to Jon and to Kevin and
to the team rather than just ordering it.

01:44:45.090 --> 01:44:49.690
And to their credit, they embraced it pretty
quickly.

01:44:49.690 --> 01:44:53.430
Which we had to do because we were pretty
close to getting to production.

01:44:53.430 --> 01:44:57.290
That, I think, made the story, we all believed,
better.

01:44:57.290 --> 01:45:00.901
We kept The Mandarin for a later film and
we saved money on the budget too because we

01:45:00.901 --> 01:45:01.901
didn't have to do that scene.

01:45:01.901 --> 01:45:03.790
And we probably didn't piss off China at the
time.

01:45:03.790 --> 01:45:05.880
So we had a lot of benefits from that decision.

01:45:05.880 --> 01:45:07.560
Tim Ferriss: Win, win, win.

01:45:07.560 --> 01:45:09.130
I don't know Robert Downey Jr.

01:45:09.130 --> 01:45:10.540
I don't know Kevin.

01:45:10.540 --> 01:45:13.170
But I have spent some time with Jon, he's
been on the podcast.

01:45:13.170 --> 01:45:21.860
And I just want to strongly plus-one that
he is one of the most talented people.

01:45:21.860 --> 01:45:24.240
And that's not to diminish the hard work that
goes into it.

01:45:24.240 --> 01:45:30.860
I should say, skilled people on a multitude
of levels that I have met.

01:45:30.860 --> 01:45:37.909
And really knows how to do what you were describing
in terms of embracing the comedy but not making

01:45:37.909 --> 01:45:47.730
it overly slapstick, and getting a lot out
of performances on screen.

01:45:47.730 --> 01:45:50.100
He's really, really exceptional.

01:45:50.100 --> 01:45:55.580
And also just very, very, at least in all
of my experiences, just a very pleasant guy

01:45:55.580 --> 01:45:56.580
to be around.

01:45:56.580 --> 01:45:57.580
David Maisel: He is.

01:45:57.580 --> 01:45:58.580
We had a blast on set.

01:45:58.580 --> 01:46:03.489
And the other thing Jon did really well, to
your point there, is we didn't freeze the

01:46:03.489 --> 01:46:05.429
script on Iron Man.

01:46:05.429 --> 01:46:10.510
Which most people would say is crazy because
each day costs three or 500,000 depending

01:46:10.510 --> 01:46:11.920
on the day of cost.

01:46:11.920 --> 01:46:17.170
So if you miss your day and you don't finish
everything, you blow that money.

01:46:17.170 --> 01:46:19.940
But we kept time for Robert to improvise.

01:46:19.940 --> 01:46:24.040
And Jon managed that and Kevin, and they did
a great job.

01:46:24.040 --> 01:46:25.040
And Jon is the director.

01:46:25.040 --> 01:46:29.800
And so many of the lines that you probably
love that so many people love came out of

01:46:29.800 --> 01:46:31.520
Robert improvising.

01:46:31.520 --> 01:46:33.489
And that was a risk.

01:46:33.489 --> 01:46:38.989
And I felt very secure in that, given Jon's
expertise, like you say.

01:46:38.989 --> 01:46:41.239
Tim Ferriss: It worked out.

01:46:41.239 --> 01:46:45.489
Let's look at the series of events.

01:46:45.489 --> 01:46:52.200
And I'd love for you to walk us through the
milestones or the key moments between Iron

01:46:52.200 --> 01:46:56.810
Man and the sale to Disney.

01:46:56.810 --> 01:47:02.040
Before we get to that, I have to ask, because
you've mentioned it a few times, why so quiet

01:47:02.040 --> 01:47:03.040
with PR?

01:47:03.040 --> 01:47:04.340
Why haven't you done more PR?

01:47:04.340 --> 01:47:09.511
David Maisel: It relates to what you're about
to ask me, the sale to Disney, which, I try

01:47:09.511 --> 01:47:11.650
to live life intentionally.

01:47:11.650 --> 01:47:21.340
And one of the things that I've tried to avoid
is to get too focused on my own, I guess,

01:47:21.340 --> 01:47:23.810
ego, for lack of a better word.

01:47:23.810 --> 01:47:32.400
And so one of the reasons I sold the company
to Disney, one big reason was shareholder

01:47:32.400 --> 01:47:34.380
value and get the best price.

01:47:34.380 --> 01:47:38.750
And the other was me, personally, I was okay
with it because I wanted to venture away and

01:47:38.750 --> 01:47:40.810
do new challenges.

01:47:40.810 --> 01:47:44.250
And we can get back to that.

01:47:44.250 --> 01:47:53.250
But I realized that if I opened the door on
the PR side, it would just be a route that

01:47:53.250 --> 01:47:58.110
didn't interest me at the time and I'd be
talking about the past all the time.

01:47:58.110 --> 01:48:02.360
I, fortunately, did well with the sale of
Marvel to Disney.

01:48:02.360 --> 01:48:08.150
And so obviously I could have afforded PR
people, and I just never did that.

01:48:08.150 --> 01:48:09.150
And that was my reason.

01:48:09.150 --> 01:48:11.730
I just thought it was unnecessary.

01:48:11.730 --> 01:48:17.170
I didn't realize, Tim, at the time, it's a
different discussion, I always thought truth

01:48:17.170 --> 01:48:18.560
always prevailed.

01:48:18.560 --> 01:48:23.310
And we've learned, in a lot of parts of our
lives in the past eight years, that that truth

01:48:23.310 --> 01:48:24.880
is fungible.

01:48:24.880 --> 01:48:31.760
And so it was something whereby being quiet,
there was a lot of confusion about all the

01:48:31.760 --> 01:48:34.230
stuff that we talked about.

01:48:34.230 --> 01:48:40.179
I found my story, essentially, being somewhat
rewritten out of history, which is really

01:48:40.179 --> 01:48:45.800
weird when it's eight years of your life and
something that was your vision and dream.

01:48:45.800 --> 01:48:52.570
And to the point of articles being written
where they just didn't even mention what we

01:48:52.570 --> 01:48:54.900
just talked about.

01:48:54.900 --> 01:49:00.420
And one famously in the cover of Vanity Fair
a few years ago.

01:49:00.420 --> 01:49:03.900
And the same author of that article is writing
a book that's coming out on the history of

01:49:03.900 --> 01:49:05.860
Marvel Studios, coming out in November.

01:49:05.860 --> 01:49:12.989
Joanna Robinson, who now has done her research
and knows the story that we've talked through

01:49:12.989 --> 01:49:16.390
and realizes the mistake that they made.

01:49:16.390 --> 01:49:20.120
So the past couple years, I've realized two
things.

01:49:20.120 --> 01:49:22.980
One, I enjoy telling the story.

01:49:22.980 --> 01:49:24.869
It's a big part of who I am.

01:49:24.869 --> 01:49:30.340
My friends knew, people like Bryan Lourd and
Kevin Feige and my Hollywood friends knew,

01:49:30.340 --> 01:49:32.929
and the people in Hollywood that mattered.

01:49:32.929 --> 01:49:38.270
But even in many cases, if they don't see
it written and so on, it gets confusing.

01:49:38.270 --> 01:49:45.500
So I've realized that if you care about something,
it's important to be part of the narrative.

01:49:45.500 --> 01:49:53.670
Luckily for Marvel, it's so big and it's done
so well that there's plenty of shine to share.

01:49:53.670 --> 01:50:00.350
As I mentioned Avi, I give him full credit
for the license deals of which I would not

01:50:00.350 --> 01:50:05.000
have been able to open some of the doors I
did.

01:50:05.000 --> 01:50:07.500
And Kevin, I'm his biggest fan.

01:50:07.500 --> 01:50:12.550
I'm proud of my vision for Marvel Studios
and where it came from me.

01:50:12.550 --> 01:50:15.540
Whether it's my future wife or it's people
listening to this podcast, that's part of

01:50:15.540 --> 01:50:16.730
who I am.

01:50:16.730 --> 01:50:20.719
I'm proud of going there and making it happen
and putting on all those different hats that

01:50:20.719 --> 01:50:23.409
we talked about that were needed at the time.

01:50:23.409 --> 01:50:29.680
And I'm proud of handing the baton to Kevin
and the sale to Disney.

01:50:29.680 --> 01:50:37.460
And so there's enough great credit for everybody
to have and hopefully everybody sees that

01:50:37.460 --> 01:50:41.310
once all the facts come out.

01:50:41.310 --> 01:50:43.850
This is a big step in talking about PR.

01:50:43.850 --> 01:50:46.560
And it's fun to tell the story to people.

01:50:46.560 --> 01:50:48.119
There's so many different angles to it.

01:50:48.119 --> 01:50:52.650
And the other part that's been a big influence
is when my mom was recently sick, a lot of

01:50:52.650 --> 01:50:57.210
the doctors and nurses and other people around
her I'd have time with and I would tell them

01:50:57.210 --> 01:51:00.290
the story, and I realized how it affected
their lives.

01:51:00.290 --> 01:51:05.780
They'd go home and tell their kids they — with
the guy who founded Marvel Studios.

01:51:05.780 --> 01:51:11.270
And not everyone gets to meet us and have
these discussions.

01:51:11.270 --> 01:51:15.520
And I realized Marvel brings a lot of joy
in people's lives.

01:51:15.520 --> 01:51:20.090
I just flew back from Miami yesterday and
I have a Marvel bag with me.

01:51:20.090 --> 01:51:28.080
The security guy at the airport was all excited
and he helped me through security, which was

01:51:28.080 --> 01:51:29.080
a good benefit.

01:51:29.080 --> 01:51:32.400
So there's actually a selfish benefit of carrying
a Marvel bag.

01:51:32.400 --> 01:51:37.540
Tim Ferriss: Well, pro-tip, get some Marvel
bags.

01:51:37.540 --> 01:51:42.630
I'm really glad that you're willing to take
the time to have this conversation.

01:51:42.630 --> 01:51:44.099
I'm having a lot of fun.

01:51:44.099 --> 01:51:45.099
I'm learning a lot.

01:51:45.099 --> 01:51:50.310
I'm taking notes not only about your entire
journey with Marvel, but I'm going to watch

01:51:50.310 --> 01:51:52.070
All That Jazz.

01:51:52.070 --> 01:51:53.760
And there's more —
David Maisel: Great.

01:51:53.760 --> 01:51:55.600
Tim Ferriss: — of course in the notes.

01:51:55.600 --> 01:51:59.310
But let's talk about Iron Man to sale.

01:51:59.310 --> 01:52:04.880
And you can walk us through that in any way
that makes sense.

01:52:04.880 --> 01:52:07.130
And I'll leave it to you to guide us.

01:52:07.130 --> 01:52:11.520
David Maisel: As we were making the film,
I mentioned that things went smoothly from

01:52:11.520 --> 01:52:14.599
spring of 2006 to May 2nd, 2008.

01:52:14.599 --> 01:52:18.811
They didn't really go smoothly in terms of
people's perception of the movie.

01:52:18.811 --> 01:52:21.690
They went smoothly in us making it, us feeling
good about it.

01:52:21.690 --> 01:52:25.620
But nobody really believed — our stock went
down for four and a half years.

01:52:25.620 --> 01:52:29.400
So I came in, my options were at 19 bucks
a share.

01:52:29.400 --> 01:52:35.170
When Iron Man came out, I think the stock
was trading at $18 a share.

01:52:35.170 --> 01:52:36.290
Not for lack of trying.

01:52:36.290 --> 01:52:41.150
I was on CNBC touting Marvel.

01:52:41.150 --> 01:52:46.130
Ike famously doesn't really care about press,
but even he agreed we should do press.

01:52:46.130 --> 01:52:50.271
And so we had articles in Businessweek at
the time and Fortune and Financial Times and

01:52:50.271 --> 01:52:51.271
all these other places.

01:52:51.271 --> 01:52:55.750
And New York Times as well, a famous article
that I keep by my office door, which talks

01:52:55.750 --> 01:52:57.650
about all the risks about Marvel.

01:52:57.650 --> 01:53:00.199
This was like a 2006 or 2007 article.

01:53:00.199 --> 01:53:06.550
And at the end of the article, I'm like, "We're
doing this really in a smart, creative way,

01:53:06.550 --> 01:53:07.671
and people are going to love it."

01:53:07.671 --> 01:53:11.310
It sounded like I was crazy on the street.

01:53:11.310 --> 01:53:15.600
So even Paramount projected we were going
to do about 40 or 50 million for the weekend.

01:53:15.600 --> 01:53:23.219
And my board came finally to L.A. They didn't
care about it really too much from that point

01:53:23.219 --> 01:53:25.590
of view, but we had the premiere.

01:53:25.590 --> 01:53:33.630
And I remember my board telling me that Kevin
and I would get, I think it was a half a million

01:53:33.630 --> 01:53:37.920
dollar bonus each if the film broke even.

01:53:37.920 --> 01:53:41.960
And so that was my own company was like, "Hey,
attaboy, way to go.

01:53:41.960 --> 01:53:47.000
Breaks even, we'll make money on the toys."

01:53:47.000 --> 01:53:48.460
I think we were immune to it.

01:53:48.460 --> 01:53:54.040
I think Kevin and I, and I think Jon and Robert,
we were so immersed in making the movie and

01:53:54.040 --> 01:54:02.909
we felt so good about it, that for some reason
that lack of confidence in the Wall Street

01:54:02.909 --> 01:54:05.800
or otherwise didn't affect us too much, but
it was there.

01:54:05.800 --> 01:54:07.540
It's important to realize that.

01:54:07.540 --> 01:54:17.030
Because I was having lunch with Bob Iger probably
once a year since I joined and Kevin Mayer,

01:54:17.030 --> 01:54:21.179
and I'd wear my Spider-Man tie and stuff like
that.

01:54:21.179 --> 01:54:26.060
But it was only after Iron Man came out did
I really start thinking, "Where does this

01:54:26.060 --> 01:54:27.510
company go?"

01:54:27.510 --> 01:54:31.500
And we had Hulk coming out a month later,
and that film, a lot of people misunderstand.

01:54:31.500 --> 01:54:33.139
They're wondering why we did it.

01:54:33.139 --> 01:54:37.199
And like I said, we did it to get the rights
back for Hulk as a supporting character in

01:54:37.199 --> 01:54:40.320
movies like The Avengers.

01:54:40.320 --> 01:54:42.210
And we had to make that movie for that.

01:54:42.210 --> 01:54:47.670
And we planned the budget of that movie to
break even, as long as it did the first film.

01:54:47.670 --> 01:54:51.830
So we weren't expecting it to break out like
Iron Man did.

01:54:51.830 --> 01:54:58.610
But the night Iron Man came out, I was at
a man named Ron Burkle's house, who had become

01:54:58.610 --> 01:55:00.790
a mentor and still is.

01:55:00.790 --> 01:55:07.230
And I got a phone call from Rob Moore at 6:00
p.m., "Hey, we're going to do 60 million."

01:55:07.230 --> 01:55:08.949
An hour later, "70 million."

01:55:08.949 --> 01:55:10.170
Every hour it went up 10 million.

01:55:10.170 --> 01:55:14.170
And finally it did 100 million at 10:00 p.m.
at night on Friday night.

01:55:14.170 --> 01:55:21.639
And I knew things had changed from Marvel,
they changed for Jon, Robert, Kevin and I.

01:55:21.639 --> 01:55:22.639
Tim Ferriss: What a night.

01:55:22.639 --> 01:55:29.660
Can I just pause to say or actually pause
to ask, what did that feel like?

01:55:29.660 --> 01:55:33.739
What was your experience as you're getting
these updates hour by hour?

01:55:33.739 --> 01:55:37.139
David Maisel: Yeah, by the way, it's still
unreal.

01:55:37.139 --> 01:55:42.540
Telling the story to you, I still pinch myself
every day that I was able to go from reading

01:55:42.540 --> 01:55:50.050
Iron Man comics to having this crazy dream,
to making Marvel into what it is today is

01:55:50.050 --> 01:55:51.050
crazy.

01:55:51.050 --> 01:55:54.590
And kids ask me, "How do you do that?"

01:55:54.590 --> 01:55:57.250
And it doesn't happen.

01:55:57.250 --> 01:56:04.060
And so when Iron Man came out and we knew
the number, the adrenaline was just huge.

01:56:04.060 --> 01:56:08.540
We had just had the premiere, people seemed
buzzed about it, but until we got that box

01:56:08.540 --> 01:56:10.270
office number — you get really busy.

01:56:10.270 --> 01:56:11.650
You have to give quotes right away.

01:56:11.650 --> 01:56:18.480
And we had a Wall Street analyst meeting coming
up that next Tuesday.

01:56:18.480 --> 01:56:24.440
And so you get busy doing things, but I probably
didn't really celebrate it till later in the

01:56:24.440 --> 01:56:26.690
summer.

01:56:26.690 --> 01:56:29.291
We did have a dinner that people might be
interested in.

01:56:29.291 --> 01:56:36.030
So I organized a dinner at Mr. Chow's, a famous
restaurant in L.A., in Beverly Hills, with

01:56:36.030 --> 01:56:40.369
Jon Favreau, Robert Downey, Kevin, and myself
to celebrate.

01:56:40.369 --> 01:56:45.210
And it was a couple of weeks after Iron Man
opened.

01:56:45.210 --> 01:56:52.560
And Kevin and I had suggested to Ike that
we buy cars for Robert and Jon as presents

01:56:52.560 --> 01:56:53.560
for them.

01:56:53.560 --> 01:56:56.760
And we had worked with their wives to figure
out what cars they want.

01:56:56.760 --> 01:57:00.710
And so Favreau had picked this Mercedes and
Robert a Bentley.

01:57:00.710 --> 01:57:04.570
And so at Mr. Chow's, we swapped Favreau's
car.

01:57:04.570 --> 01:57:09.750
So he drove in with whatever old car he had,
and we knew he was going to get his Mercedes.

01:57:09.750 --> 01:57:11.840
And we brought Robert in on the joke.

01:57:11.840 --> 01:57:14.280
So I'll come back to that in a second.

01:57:14.280 --> 01:57:20.880
But at dinner we celebrated and I said, "Guys,
I will greenlight Iron Man 2 if everyone around

01:57:20.880 --> 01:57:25.140
this table..." and I looked at Jon and Robert
and Kevin, "...commit to making it so we can

01:57:25.140 --> 01:57:30.380
release it May 2nd, 2010," or whatever the
equivalent weekend was, which was really quick

01:57:30.380 --> 01:57:32.429
for a movie with no script.

01:57:32.429 --> 01:57:36.679
And I think everyone was sort of surprised.

01:57:36.679 --> 01:57:38.270
Things don't just happen that way in Hollywood.

01:57:38.270 --> 01:57:41.909
So a lot of what we did in this movie was
like film school.

01:57:41.909 --> 01:57:46.869
I mean, Natalie Portman I was introduced to
by Leo DiCaprio.

01:57:46.869 --> 01:57:48.989
We wanted Natalie to be in Thor.

01:57:48.989 --> 01:57:53.630
She had no desire, Kevin Huvane was her agent,
to really be in it.

01:57:53.630 --> 01:57:57.929
And then he knew I wanted her in the movie
and he was bowling with her one night and

01:57:57.929 --> 01:58:02.810
texted me to get dressed and come out to a
midnight bowling in Hollywood.

01:58:02.810 --> 01:58:07.590
And he introduced me to her and said, "You
two should work together."

01:58:07.590 --> 01:58:11.800
So it was really like kids in film school.

01:58:11.800 --> 01:58:14.560
So it all worked out for everybody.

01:58:14.560 --> 01:58:15.590
So we did celebrate.

01:58:15.590 --> 01:58:19.429
And then at the end of that night, we gave
Jon his new car.

01:58:19.429 --> 01:58:25.040
But the funniest part was Robert was in on
the joke, but there was no car for him.

01:58:25.040 --> 01:58:28.210
And we did that on purpose so that he'd get
like, "Where the hell's my car?"

01:58:28.210 --> 01:58:29.840
in his mind.

01:58:29.840 --> 01:58:31.800
And so we waited a week, so he'd be wondering.

01:58:31.800 --> 01:58:33.170
Tim Ferriss: Oh, brutal, brutal.

01:58:33.170 --> 01:58:34.170
David Maisel: Yeah.

01:58:34.170 --> 01:58:38.320
His wife told us when he was taking a nap
one afternoon and we brought it into his driveway,

01:58:38.320 --> 01:58:41.650
so when he woke up, he saw it in his driveway,
which was fun.

01:58:41.650 --> 01:58:42.980
Tim Ferriss: That's incredible.

01:58:42.980 --> 01:58:43.980
That's so fun.

01:58:43.980 --> 01:58:45.670
David Maisel: So we had fun with it a bit.

01:58:45.670 --> 01:58:46.670
Yeah.

01:58:46.670 --> 01:58:52.290
Tim Ferriss: So you mentioned these regular
meals, I think it was with Bob Iger.

01:58:52.290 --> 01:58:58.030
And then you mentioned Ron Burkle, who has
become and still is a mentor.

01:58:58.030 --> 01:59:00.469
I want to pick up on those two.

01:59:00.469 --> 01:59:06.780
What was the context of meeting with Bob on
a regular basis?

01:59:06.780 --> 01:59:14.060
Was it just to keep in touch, to swap stories,
to give news from the front lines in your

01:59:14.060 --> 01:59:15.619
respective worlds?

01:59:15.619 --> 01:59:16.790
What was the context?

01:59:16.790 --> 01:59:20.810
David Maisel: In a stepping back for your
audience.

01:59:20.810 --> 01:59:22.610
It wasn't just having meals.

01:59:22.610 --> 01:59:26.320
I called Bob a couple of times with business
proposals.

01:59:26.320 --> 01:59:29.930
One of the times, I said, "Let's do animated
movies.

01:59:29.930 --> 01:59:32.020
50-50 Marvel, Disney."

01:59:32.020 --> 01:59:33.070
And that didn't go anywhere.

01:59:33.070 --> 01:59:38.860
And so we had a habit of talking about business
opportunities, but our stock was so low in

01:59:38.860 --> 01:59:45.630
February of 2009 that I didn't raise a sale
at all.

01:59:45.630 --> 01:59:50.560
And then importantly, and this is all public,
because when you do things as a public company,

01:59:50.560 --> 01:59:55.810
the SEC makes you go back for five years,
every email, every meeting, every communication

01:59:55.810 --> 02:00:01.340
I had with Bob, with Kevin Mayer or anybody
else at Disney is public record.

02:00:01.340 --> 02:00:04.070
But I called Bob at the end of May.

02:00:04.070 --> 02:00:06.300
Our stock was back up to 35.

02:00:06.300 --> 02:00:09.840
I felt that Disney stock was also undervalued.

02:00:09.840 --> 02:00:13.280
It was at $25 a share with the economic collapse.

02:00:13.280 --> 02:00:17.630
And Disney, as a company, is very sensitive
to the economy.

02:00:17.630 --> 02:00:21.480
People go to theme parks more often, they
do stuff like that.

02:00:21.480 --> 02:00:27.860
And so I felt like I was a big Disney shareholder,
or I follow the stock closely at least, that

02:00:27.860 --> 02:00:31.560
Disney was at least a double when the economy
recovered.

02:00:31.560 --> 02:00:37.489
Whereas we were at two movies a year at best,
and could be plateaued for a while.

02:00:37.489 --> 02:00:40.960
And Dreamworks Animation was a company that
also had two movies a year, and they were

02:00:40.960 --> 02:00:41.960
plateaued.

02:00:41.960 --> 02:00:48.770
So all of that in my mind, when I saw our
stock hit 35 or 36 or 37, I called Bob, we

02:00:48.770 --> 02:00:52.170
organized a secret meeting in his office for
early June.

02:00:52.170 --> 02:00:55.940
I brought my Marvel encyclopedia of characters
with me.

02:00:55.940 --> 02:01:01.320
And that's the meeting where — this is news
for your listeners.

02:01:01.320 --> 02:01:04.880
There was two deals that we had on the table
that would've encumbered us.

02:01:04.880 --> 02:01:09.969
Encumbering means we would've tied up our
rights, and been way less attractive for a

02:01:09.969 --> 02:01:10.969
potential purchaser.

02:01:10.969 --> 02:01:16.061
We were about to extend our distribution deal
on the movies beyond what Paramount had to

02:01:16.061 --> 02:01:17.061
another studio.

02:01:17.061 --> 02:01:19.800
I won't name them on this thing yet.

02:01:19.800 --> 02:01:25.870
But we were very close to doing another 10
or 20 movies to them.

02:01:25.870 --> 02:01:31.040
And we were about to do a kids' TV network
with Discovery and Hasbro, which ended up

02:01:31.040 --> 02:01:32.280
being called The Hub.

02:01:32.280 --> 02:01:37.000
So I remember David Zaslav at the time, and
Brian Goldner, the CEO of Hasbro, recently

02:01:37.000 --> 02:01:38.000
passed away.

02:01:38.000 --> 02:01:40.980
The three of us were working on this, and
I was working on this other deal.

02:01:40.980 --> 02:01:46.139
So I knew that there was a window to sell
the company, that six months later we'd be

02:01:46.139 --> 02:01:49.770
way less attractive to Disney or anyone else
because we'd be committed to somebody else

02:01:49.770 --> 02:01:53.020
in TV and then somebody else in film.

02:01:53.020 --> 02:01:54.150
And I was gun shy.

02:01:54.150 --> 02:01:59.190
I knew how easily a deal can fall apart from
my previous time suggesting this at Disney.

02:01:59.190 --> 02:02:03.920
So all of that was in my mind in June when
I went to see Bob.

02:02:03.920 --> 02:02:08.440
And I remember telling Bob about our TV deal
with Discovery and Hasbro.

02:02:08.440 --> 02:02:11.370
And him saying, "Hold on, let's do a TV deal
with Disney.

02:02:11.370 --> 02:02:19.570
And we'll do a primarily male but kids, teenager
entertainment network."

02:02:19.570 --> 02:02:23.950
And I joked with him, I said, "No, not going
to get a little bit pregnant.

02:02:23.950 --> 02:02:26.340
We either do something big or we don't."

02:02:26.340 --> 02:02:31.790
And he took that, to his credit, and said,
"Then let's think something big."

02:02:31.790 --> 02:02:36.550
And I remember vividly him saying — I said,
"I think we would need $50 a share.

02:02:36.550 --> 02:02:37.800
We need a five in front of it."

02:02:37.800 --> 02:02:39.699
So we went pretty far in this meeting.

02:02:39.699 --> 02:02:45.460
And he knew I knew Kevin Mayer, and he knew
I knew Tom Staggs, who was CFO at the time,

02:02:45.460 --> 02:02:46.460
I think.

02:02:46.460 --> 02:02:50.570
And he basically said, "Will my board and
Kevin and Tom think that's a fair price?"

02:02:50.570 --> 02:02:51.599
And I said, "Absolutely."

02:02:51.599 --> 02:02:55.760
I said, "I've got this thing planned out for
the next 10 years."

02:02:55.760 --> 02:03:00.110
And so since I had done work for Bob, he,
I think, had some trust in that.

02:03:00.110 --> 02:03:03.929
And we talked about the vision of connecting
all the characters and what the future movies

02:03:03.929 --> 02:03:05.239
were going to be.

02:03:05.239 --> 02:03:08.690
Iron Man 2 was our next movie.

02:03:08.690 --> 02:03:13.719
We had delayed Thor, so I knew that would
be good to have Iron Man 2 as the next movie

02:03:13.719 --> 02:03:16.460
because that's predictable.

02:03:16.460 --> 02:03:20.171
And I explained how we're leading up to The
Avengers, and how everything after Iron Man

02:03:20.171 --> 02:03:21.730
would be a sequel to Iron Man.

02:03:21.730 --> 02:03:26.900
Everything after Avengers is a sequel to Avengers,
which I think has proved to be the case.

02:03:26.900 --> 02:03:31.429
And that was first week of June, 2009.

02:03:31.429 --> 02:03:37.770
We announced the deal the end of August, two
months later, and we kept it secret.

02:03:37.770 --> 02:03:42.650
And they ended up paying, I think it was 51
a share, and we got 40 percent of it in Disney

02:03:42.650 --> 02:03:43.790
stock.

02:03:43.790 --> 02:03:46.270
We wanted 50 percent.

02:03:46.270 --> 02:03:51.790
And so that was a great trade for our shareholders,
and turned out to be one.

02:03:51.790 --> 02:03:54.930
And Disney got up to 180 or 200 at some point.

02:03:54.930 --> 02:03:59.929
Now they're close to 100 even with their correction.

02:03:59.929 --> 02:04:03.639
And so I remember meeting with Kevin Mayer
right after that.

02:04:03.639 --> 02:04:05.020
We didn't even have a banker.

02:04:05.020 --> 02:04:09.940
And I went through my projections with Kevin
and as friends, we yelled at each other, swore

02:04:09.940 --> 02:04:13.909
at each other, all that kind of stuff.

02:04:13.909 --> 02:04:20.840
I was basically saying, "For Captain America
and Thor, you need to give us credit for 80

02:04:20.840 --> 02:04:23.310
percent of Iron Man in your model."

02:04:23.310 --> 02:04:25.739
And he's like, "No, it should be 60 percent.

02:04:25.739 --> 02:04:27.949
No one cares about those characters," whatever.

02:04:27.949 --> 02:04:32.540
And then I said, "Iron Man 2 should be like
110 percent of Iron Man, and Avengers should

02:04:32.540 --> 02:04:34.590
be like 150 percent of Iron Man."

02:04:34.590 --> 02:04:37.250
He's like, "No, Avengers is a new movie.

02:04:37.250 --> 02:04:41.040
That should be like Captain America or Thor."

02:04:41.040 --> 02:04:42.310
And I was like, "No."

02:04:42.310 --> 02:04:48.460
Robert Downey we had signed up for The Avengers,
so it's basically a quasi-Iron Man sequel.

02:04:48.460 --> 02:04:54.040
It's an interesting story because I'm negotiating
with someone I worked with that I've known

02:04:54.040 --> 02:04:58.810
for decades with a company where I worked
at intimately.

02:04:58.810 --> 02:05:05.530
And to your question though, after that first
meeting, I went to that meeting, Ike didn't

02:05:05.530 --> 02:05:08.460
even know I was having a meeting.

02:05:08.460 --> 02:05:13.360
And if he did know, he would not be surprised
because it would be about a business deal,

02:05:13.360 --> 02:05:14.360
that kind of stuff.

02:05:14.360 --> 02:05:19.290
I called Ike from in front of Team Disney
after leaving Bob.

02:05:19.290 --> 02:05:24.340
And I was as pumped about this meeting as
I was about Iron Man's box office because

02:05:24.340 --> 02:05:26.630
I could see the excitement in Bob's eyes.

02:05:26.630 --> 02:05:30.120
It turns out that they had internally been
reviewing options, and Marvel was on their

02:05:30.120 --> 02:05:34.920
list, though they hadn't proactively contacted
anybody, including myself.

02:05:34.920 --> 02:05:39.020
And I called Ike and I told him that I think
I have a buyer for the company.

02:05:39.020 --> 02:05:44.310
And I thought there'd be one of three responses,
Tim.

02:05:44.310 --> 02:05:49.360
Even though Iron Man had came out, even though
it was my idea to do the MCU or this new Marvel

02:05:49.360 --> 02:05:54.220
Studios self-financed, self-producing and
connecting the characters, I want to come

02:05:54.220 --> 02:05:59.940
back to it in a second, but even with all
that, I knew Ike might say, "You're fired,"

02:05:59.940 --> 02:06:01.760
right on the spot.

02:06:01.760 --> 02:06:07.230
Or more likely, "Get your ass back to the
office and work on Iron Man 2," and other

02:06:07.230 --> 02:06:11.020
initiatives that we were doing as a company.

02:06:11.020 --> 02:06:15.010
But he said, "Fly to New York, come see me."

02:06:15.010 --> 02:06:20.200
And I made a reservation to come right away,
like the next morning or whatever.

02:06:20.200 --> 02:06:25.950
And I wrote on the airplane what my scenario
planning would be, and so he was open-minded

02:06:25.950 --> 02:06:29.110
to it once I got there and made the case.

02:06:29.110 --> 02:06:30.350
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

02:06:30.350 --> 02:06:31.350
Okay.

02:06:31.350 --> 02:06:35.080
So he didn't lop your head off, didn't march
you off with the guards.

02:06:35.080 --> 02:06:36.080
David Maisel: No.

02:06:36.080 --> 02:06:44.070
Tim Ferriss: Well, that seems to speak volumes
to his character and presence of mind to be

02:06:44.070 --> 02:06:52.429
able to mentally, having committed for so
long to not selling, nor even entertaining

02:06:52.429 --> 02:07:00.880
the possibility of a sale, to endure the cognitive
dissonance of considering it as an option.

02:07:00.880 --> 02:07:03.380
I mean, that's no small feat.

02:07:03.380 --> 02:07:06.980
David Maisel: Yeah, and I think that's a trait
I saw in Ike all the way through.

02:07:06.980 --> 02:07:11.630
He's notorious for being very fiscally conservative,
and some people mistake that for not being

02:07:11.630 --> 02:07:14.409
supportive of major decisions.

02:07:14.409 --> 02:07:18.930
And he did hire me and gave me a chance with
Marvel Studios.

02:07:18.930 --> 02:07:20.090
And here he gave me a chance.

02:07:20.090 --> 02:07:23.360
He still wasn't convinced, but he at least
wanted to hear my case.

02:07:23.360 --> 02:07:25.730
And it took a while to convince him.

02:07:25.730 --> 02:07:30.020
And then the major thing was Bob and Ike had
to get together.

02:07:30.020 --> 02:07:34.630
And so in the meeting with Bob, I remember
Bob said, "What do we need to do?"

02:07:34.630 --> 02:07:37.929
He even asked me, "What do you want out of
this, David?"

02:07:37.929 --> 02:07:40.840
And I said, "No, no, you have to make Ike
happy.

02:07:40.840 --> 02:07:44.090
That's the person that has to be convinced."

02:07:44.090 --> 02:07:53.050
And I had basically understood from Bob that
if Ike didn't want to do something that this

02:07:53.050 --> 02:07:55.170
wouldn't go anywhere.

02:07:55.170 --> 02:08:00.920
And so I arranged a meeting in New York, where
Bob came to the offices at nine o'clock at

02:08:00.920 --> 02:08:01.920
night.

02:08:01.920 --> 02:08:03.130
And he and Ike, I let them go to dinner.

02:08:03.130 --> 02:08:06.040
I kept back and I didn't ask to go.

02:08:06.040 --> 02:08:07.310
And they had a wonderful dinner.

02:08:07.310 --> 02:08:14.190
And I think this is public, Bob had Steve
Jobs call Ike and talked to him how well Bob

02:08:14.190 --> 02:08:17.770
preserved Pixar's culture after the sale.

02:08:17.770 --> 02:08:22.130
And so all of that really helped get this
across the finish line and — yeah.

02:08:22.130 --> 02:08:24.060
Tim Ferriss: Incredible.

02:08:24.060 --> 02:08:25.060
What a story.

02:08:25.060 --> 02:08:26.060
Wow, what a wild adventure.

02:08:26.060 --> 02:08:33.540
How does Ron Burkle fit in as your mentor
in all of this?

02:08:33.540 --> 02:08:39.960
And maybe just in a snapshot, explain just
enough background so that people can conjure

02:08:39.960 --> 02:08:41.150
an image of someone.

02:08:41.150 --> 02:08:45.500
David Maisel: Ron is most famous for owning
Ralphs grocery stores.

02:08:45.500 --> 02:08:50.730
He started as a bagger and became the owner,
and became a billionaire through that and

02:08:50.730 --> 02:08:51.810
other investments.

02:08:51.810 --> 02:08:59.380
And he's notorious as a very savvy investor
and a very hard businessman in terms of the

02:08:59.380 --> 02:09:05.190
terms that he requests for investments.

02:09:05.190 --> 02:09:10.620
I don't know exactly where we met, but I think
we met at one point when he was interested

02:09:10.620 --> 02:09:16.630
in buying Endeavor when I was working with
Ari in the early days.

02:09:16.630 --> 02:09:20.650
And that deal didn't go anywhere, but Ron
and I became friends.

02:09:20.650 --> 02:09:26.520
And then he watched my career at Marvel and
the relationship grew deeper.

02:09:26.520 --> 02:09:33.469
We had Leo as a mutual friend, and so we had
social acquaintances in common as well.

02:09:33.469 --> 02:09:37.199
But no, the intersection with the Marvel story
is I was at his house for a party that night

02:09:37.199 --> 02:09:39.690
when the box office came out.

02:09:39.690 --> 02:09:44.010
The night before Iron Man came out, Kevin
and Robert and Jon and I went to theaters

02:09:44.010 --> 02:09:48.251
together to see the Thursday night shows,
and that was a blast because it was the first

02:09:48.251 --> 02:09:51.219
time we saw a live audience watching the films.

02:09:51.219 --> 02:09:53.060
We didn't test market these movies at all.

02:09:53.060 --> 02:09:54.190
And yeah, that was exciting.

02:09:54.190 --> 02:09:57.280
We wanted Robert to sell tickets, but they
wouldn't let us have him do that.

02:09:57.280 --> 02:09:59.500
Tim Ferriss: Why didn't you test market it?

02:09:59.500 --> 02:10:00.500
I'm curious.

02:10:00.500 --> 02:10:01.500
So no test audiences?

02:10:01.500 --> 02:10:03.239
David Maisel: It's very atypical.

02:10:03.239 --> 02:10:04.889
We could have really blown this, Tim.

02:10:04.889 --> 02:10:09.090
I mean, yeah, we went a lot with our instincts
and the collective group.

02:10:09.090 --> 02:10:12.489
And I'm trying to remember if we ever had
one.

02:10:12.489 --> 02:10:13.960
I don't recall it happening.

02:10:13.960 --> 02:10:14.960
No.

02:10:14.960 --> 02:10:16.139
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

02:10:16.139 --> 02:10:25.730
So you mentioned the dinner, the turning point
dinner with Bob and Ike, and then a lesser-known

02:10:25.730 --> 02:10:31.660
fellow named Steve Jobs calling Ike to talk
about Pixar.

02:10:31.660 --> 02:10:39.040
And then we know how that story, in a sense,
ends, at least from the public perspective,

02:10:39.040 --> 02:10:41.460
which is in the successful acquisition.

02:10:41.460 --> 02:10:48.619
Are there any other points along that path
that you would like to mention or fill in?

02:10:48.619 --> 02:10:49.619
David Maisel: Yeah.

02:10:49.619 --> 02:10:55.380
I think from a deal perspective, besides negotiating
the percentage in Disney stock, we were pretty

02:10:55.380 --> 02:10:59.059
close to what Bob and I spoke to in that first
meeting.

02:10:59.059 --> 02:11:04.050
And then it became about keeping Marvel as
a separate company within Disney, so the culture

02:11:04.050 --> 02:11:05.780
didn't get messed up.

02:11:05.780 --> 02:11:14.730
And I specifically wanted to step aside and
I introduced, I told Bob about Kevin, how

02:11:14.730 --> 02:11:21.290
I thought he could run the Marvel entity within
Disney in a uniquely great way.

02:11:21.290 --> 02:11:25.190
And then, Bob got to meet Kevin and see for
himself.

02:11:25.190 --> 02:11:31.329
And so that was a big part because personally
I wanted to go on to different ventures and

02:11:31.329 --> 02:11:33.040
different parts of my life.

02:11:33.040 --> 02:11:38.840
And selling to Disney allowed me to do that
in a very clean way, both financially with

02:11:38.840 --> 02:11:42.120
other shareholders to be able to afford that.

02:11:42.120 --> 02:11:45.920
And then two, easier to leave a company when
it's a division of a bigger company than by

02:11:45.920 --> 02:11:46.920
itself.

02:11:46.920 --> 02:11:52.380
And so, that for me was, personally, in addition
to the deal time, it was sort of exciting

02:11:52.380 --> 02:11:57.310
to think about my next venture and scary,
because I didn't quite know who I was outside

02:11:57.310 --> 02:12:03.260
of my role at Marvel and what that person
would be.

02:12:03.260 --> 02:12:09.530
But besides that, the deal went through pretty
well.

02:12:09.530 --> 02:12:17.770
And the price of it, we got $4 billion, which
was about 3.8 billion more than the price

02:12:17.770 --> 02:12:19.240
when I joined.

02:12:19.240 --> 02:12:24.520
And we ran a lot of scenarios when I presented
to the board in 2004.

02:12:24.520 --> 02:12:30.679
But the main scenario valued the new studio
as potentially in the three to four billion

02:12:30.679 --> 02:12:31.790
dollar range.

02:12:31.790 --> 02:12:35.530
And it came out close enough to that.

02:12:35.530 --> 02:12:40.770
And our comic division and other things had
not really grown too much.

02:12:40.770 --> 02:12:46.510
Our toy division, we went from making toys
to licensing, which sounds like a minor thing,

02:12:46.510 --> 02:12:49.180
but it was big at the time.

02:12:49.180 --> 02:12:54.699
But pretty much it was — a lot of people
now, I guess, are hearing Marvel Studios was

02:12:54.699 --> 02:12:57.699
a startup within the parent company of Marvel.

02:12:57.699 --> 02:12:59.880
But it was one that had to raise its own money.

02:12:59.880 --> 02:13:02.309
It got the IP from the parent company.

02:13:02.309 --> 02:13:06.530
But other than that, we had to manage it,
raise our own money, do our own deals.

02:13:06.530 --> 02:13:11.889
And I was speaking to a tech entrepreneur
this past weekend in Florida from one of the

02:13:11.889 --> 02:13:13.329
biggest tech companies in the world.

02:13:13.329 --> 02:13:18.849
And he was very intrigued about how Marvel
Studios was a startup just like his company

02:13:18.849 --> 02:13:21.210
was at the time.

02:13:21.210 --> 02:13:23.889
And so, hopefully, that's interesting to a
lot of people that are hearing this.

02:13:23.889 --> 02:13:32.900
Tim Ferriss: So David, I think this may be
a logical place to begin to wind to a close

02:13:32.900 --> 02:13:38.090
with this first chapter of our ongoing conversation.

02:13:38.090 --> 02:13:39.550
David Maisel: Okay.

02:13:39.550 --> 02:13:42.730
Tim Ferriss: And I want to do a bit of teasing.

02:13:42.730 --> 02:13:46.730
So next time, there's lots I would want to
talk about.

02:13:46.730 --> 02:13:52.960
But broadly speaking, I'd want to talk about
some of your adventures outside of Marvel

02:13:52.960 --> 02:13:53.960
and Disney.

02:13:53.960 --> 02:14:01.650
So Angry Birds and other things, including
Ekos Genesis, which we will talk about at

02:14:01.650 --> 02:14:03.270
some length.

02:14:03.270 --> 02:14:13.340
And also how people might think about building
a universe, constructing a universe of characters.

02:14:13.340 --> 02:14:19.570
What are the necessary steps, common mistakes,
et cetera, that you might be able to share

02:14:19.570 --> 02:14:23.210
as lessons learned or things observed.

02:14:23.210 --> 02:14:32.210
And I would say, I'll ask you two of my favorite
rapid fire questions.

02:14:32.210 --> 02:14:36.560
The answers don't need to be rapid fire, but
the questions are intended to be short because

02:14:36.560 --> 02:14:41.449
I can be very long-winded with my 17-part
questions.

02:14:41.449 --> 02:14:43.360
This lead up is an example of that.

02:14:43.360 --> 02:14:52.130
So the first question is, what books, if any,
have you gifted the most to other people or

02:14:52.130 --> 02:14:55.130
recommended the most, that come to mind?

02:14:55.130 --> 02:14:58.400
Or gifted a lot or recommended a lot to other
people?

02:14:58.400 --> 02:15:01.790
Are there any books that come to mind?

02:15:01.790 --> 02:15:08.590
David Maisel: The book I recommend to people
the most is Stranger In A Strange Land by

02:15:08.590 --> 02:15:09.590
Robert Heinlein.

02:15:09.590 --> 02:15:10.590
But I'm not sure that's —
Tim Ferriss: Such a good book.

02:15:10.590 --> 02:15:11.840
David Maisel: Yeah, I'm not sure that's really
—

02:15:11.840 --> 02:15:12.840
Tim Ferriss: That counts.

02:15:12.840 --> 02:15:13.840
That counts.

02:15:13.840 --> 02:15:14.840
David Maisel: Yeah.

02:15:14.840 --> 02:15:15.840
Just because I think it's —
Tim Ferriss: That's a classic.

02:15:15.840 --> 02:15:16.840
David Maisel: — awesome.

02:15:16.840 --> 02:15:17.840
Yeah, and it's —
Tim Ferriss: It's an incredible book.

02:15:17.840 --> 02:15:21.320
David Maisel: It's probably the live action
movie I would most like to make as an individual

02:15:21.320 --> 02:15:22.320
movie.

02:15:22.320 --> 02:15:23.320
You know what I mean?

02:15:23.320 --> 02:15:24.320
Tim Ferriss: Hm.

02:15:24.320 --> 02:15:25.320
David Maisel: Which could be interesting.

02:15:25.320 --> 02:15:26.320
Tim Ferriss: Oh, my God.

02:15:26.320 --> 02:15:27.320
David Maisel: Yeah.

02:15:27.320 --> 02:15:28.320
Tim Ferriss: That could be interesting.

02:15:28.320 --> 02:15:29.320
All right.

02:15:29.320 --> 02:15:30.320
Tim Ferriss: Let me ask you maybe a close
cousin of a question, which is not one of

02:15:30.320 --> 02:15:31.320
my rapid fire.

02:15:31.320 --> 02:15:32.320
David Maisel: Yeah.

02:15:32.320 --> 02:15:38.369
Tim Ferriss: But are there any properties,
characters, stories that you are not thinking

02:15:38.369 --> 02:15:39.369
of pursuing yourself?

02:15:39.369 --> 02:15:40.369
David Maisel: Yeah.

02:15:40.369 --> 02:15:46.559
Tim Ferriss: That you just hope someone excellent
out there makes into a film or some type of

02:15:46.559 --> 02:15:49.410
immersive world outside of its original format?

02:15:49.410 --> 02:15:52.900
David Maisel: I love how you said film or
some other type of immersive world.

02:15:52.900 --> 02:15:54.640
I think that's very savvy, by the way.

02:15:54.640 --> 02:15:59.590
And we can get into that in the next discussion,
because I don't believe launching the next

02:15:59.590 --> 02:16:03.750
universe is going to happen like Marvel did
through theatrical film.

02:16:03.750 --> 02:16:10.310
I think it's going to start with something
else, and most likely will, because of the

02:16:10.310 --> 02:16:11.670
way the world has changed.

02:16:11.670 --> 02:16:16.420
And so I think the question in the topic,
as a tease for this next thing, when I left

02:16:16.420 --> 02:16:21.900
Marvel, I had to figure out who I was from
a person point of view.

02:16:21.900 --> 02:16:25.030
Would people still want to talk to me if I
wasn't running Marvel Studios, right?

02:16:25.030 --> 02:16:29.460
Would I still be able to go to restaurants
and things like that?

02:16:29.460 --> 02:16:30.840
Who my real friends were?

02:16:30.840 --> 02:16:35.080
And then, from a business point of view, what
would catch my eye?

02:16:35.080 --> 02:16:40.750
And what I'd say is, because of Marvel within
Disney and the power of the two together,

02:16:40.750 --> 02:16:46.840
and because of how well Kevin has run Marvel
since, Marvel's gotten so big that the bar

02:16:46.840 --> 02:16:54.519
to launch a new universe has gotten so high
and so much tougher because now you're competing

02:16:54.519 --> 02:16:56.639
against Marvel, right?

02:16:56.639 --> 02:17:04.460
So when I launched in 2008, Iron Man, we had
DC as a competitor, we had the Marvel-licensed

02:17:04.460 --> 02:17:08.939
movies, Fox with X-Men, Sony with Spider-Man.

02:17:08.939 --> 02:17:11.820
They were not just competitors, they could
use the Marvel brand.

02:17:11.820 --> 02:17:14.370
So they were weird competitors.

02:17:14.370 --> 02:17:16.820
We were scared about all these people.

02:17:16.820 --> 02:17:20.649
But in retrospect, we had a relatively open
playing field because we didn't have ourselves

02:17:20.649 --> 02:17:25.240
to compete with, the version of Marvel today,
which just dominates so much.

02:17:25.240 --> 02:17:30.460
And we didn't have streaming, we didn't have
as much social media, all these other distractions

02:17:30.460 --> 02:17:31.540
on people's time.

02:17:31.540 --> 02:17:38.710
And it was tough, but it's like an old saying,
"How good we had it, we didn't realize."

02:17:38.710 --> 02:17:41.920
Still very tough, but now it's even harder.

02:17:41.920 --> 02:17:48.960
So something that goes through the filter
you're talking about has to be pretty special

02:17:48.960 --> 02:17:55.860
to hop into what we call a universe category,
or major, a potentially new mythology.

02:17:55.860 --> 02:17:59.760
And I'd love to talk about that more because
it is something that a lot of people do ask

02:17:59.760 --> 02:18:02.690
about and a lot of people are focused on.

02:18:02.690 --> 02:18:10.010
Obviously, DC just hired a new person to run
DC after 15 years or 20 years of us being

02:18:10.010 --> 02:18:12.649
able to do what we did at Marvel.

02:18:12.649 --> 02:18:17.769
David Zaslav, when he bought Warner Brothers
from AT&amp;T, said, "I want to copy Marvel, I

02:18:17.769 --> 02:18:19.670
want to do what Marvel did."

02:18:19.670 --> 02:18:22.300
But we did that 15, 20 years ago.

02:18:22.300 --> 02:18:27.340
So it shows you even DC hasn't gotten their
act together in the same way.

02:18:27.340 --> 02:18:31.240
And that's a very well-capitalized company
with a large number of properties.

02:18:31.240 --> 02:18:38.420
So it's very hard for a new universe to get
created.

02:18:38.420 --> 02:18:43.540
I don't think it's impossible, but it's got
to be differentiated from Marvel so that there's

02:18:43.540 --> 02:18:45.070
a reason that it exists.

02:18:45.070 --> 02:18:52.000
I think it has to be introduced in new ways
that you sort of tease with new technologies

02:18:52.000 --> 02:18:58.540
and new ways of storytelling, probably has
to be way more primal and visual so it gets

02:18:58.540 --> 02:18:59.830
people's quick attention.

02:18:59.830 --> 02:19:00.920
They can see what's there.

02:19:00.920 --> 02:19:04.490
I think it has to have great meaning, Tim.

02:19:04.490 --> 02:19:08.019
Marvel had a good meaning in that it's great
to be a hero.

02:19:08.019 --> 02:19:15.670
And I think people relate to something that
they feel is adding to their lives in somehow,

02:19:15.670 --> 02:19:19.139
whether they sense it or it's just subconscious.

02:19:19.139 --> 02:19:23.130
That's what all mythologies from Greek and
Roman onward have done, or before even Greek

02:19:23.130 --> 02:19:25.790
and Roman mythology.

02:19:25.790 --> 02:19:32.599
And it has to be something, I think — listen,
James Cameron with Avatar has probably done

02:19:32.599 --> 02:19:37.229
the best since Marvel came out with big cultural
moments.

02:19:37.229 --> 02:19:40.830
Now he does things every 10 years though,
right?

02:19:40.830 --> 02:19:43.240
But that was an original idea.

02:19:43.240 --> 02:19:50.640
I think the next thing probably is a legacy
property, but it is still, like Marvel, it

02:19:50.640 --> 02:19:53.950
had history, but people could discover it
together.

02:19:53.950 --> 02:19:57.069
So it was the perfect combination.

02:19:57.069 --> 02:20:02.461
And I think people love to discover something
with their friends, and the idea of the new

02:20:02.461 --> 02:20:06.990
band or something in new technology, there's
just something great.

02:20:06.990 --> 02:20:11.850
I hear you and Kevin talking on podcasts,
like two friends discovering things together,

02:20:11.850 --> 02:20:14.640
whether it be pipe smoking or whether it be
something else.

02:20:14.640 --> 02:20:16.399
And it's like, yeah.

02:20:16.399 --> 02:20:17.620
But there's something fun.

02:20:17.620 --> 02:20:18.890
Pipe smoking's a good example.

02:20:18.890 --> 02:20:24.189
That's a legacy activity that we understand
our grandparents did and people before them.

02:20:24.189 --> 02:20:26.700
But it still felt new when you talked about
it with Kevin.

02:20:26.700 --> 02:20:27.700
You know?

02:20:27.700 --> 02:20:28.700
Tim Ferriss: Yep.

02:20:28.700 --> 02:20:29.700
For sure.

02:20:29.700 --> 02:20:30.700
David Maisel: And Marvel was legacy.

02:20:30.700 --> 02:20:31.790
But for most people, they never read the comics.

02:20:31.790 --> 02:20:33.310
It was new for them to talk with their friends.

02:20:33.310 --> 02:20:34.310
"Did you see Iron Man?

02:20:34.310 --> 02:20:35.310
Did you see all that?"

02:20:35.310 --> 02:20:36.310
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

02:20:36.310 --> 02:20:37.310
David Maisel: And so —
Tim Ferriss: It's like the youngsters these

02:20:37.310 --> 02:20:38.350
days running around with Nirvana shirts on.

02:20:38.350 --> 02:20:39.350
David Maisel: Yeah.

02:20:39.350 --> 02:20:42.050
Tim Ferriss: I mean, it's really been this
resurgence of grunge popularity.

02:20:42.050 --> 02:20:44.310
